AB  - Satellite-derived Urban Surface Temperature (SUST) is a key variable in urban practical applications. Therefore, improving spatial resolution and accuracy in calculating SUST at sub-pixel scales in heterogeneous areas is essential for capturing spatial variations of UST corresponding to changes in 2D and 3D factors. The aim of this study is to provide a framework for advanced mapping of Landsat-derived Downscaled SUST (DSUST) with super resolution by integrating multi-dimensional features. Using a Random Forest algorithm, a model is developed to predict a 1-m DSUST map. The average r between DSUST and mean radiant temperature (Tmrt), obtained from the SOLWEIG model, was 0.885 for the warm season and 0.915 for the cold season. The r and RMSE between SUST and modeled UST were 0.88 (0.90), and 1.21 K (0.82 K) for warm (cold) seasons, respectively. Evaluation of the 1-m DSUST maps confirmed previously observed thermal patterns, such as the cooling effect of shadows (up to 6.5 °C) and the elevated surface temperatures on rooftops due to high Sky View Factor (SVF) and solar exposure. However, the novelty lies in the fine-scale quantification of these differences across highly heterogeneous surfaces at 1-m resolution something not achievable with coarser data. For instance, the 1-m maps revealed that vegetated surfaces can be up to 7 °C cooler than nearby impervious areas, emphasizing the utility of ultra-high-resolution modeling for capturing microclimatic thermal variations relevant to urban design and heat mitigation. Areas shaded by tall buildings, especially in the warm season, were predominantly classified as “Excellent” thermal conditions, while building rooftops were categorized as “Worse” and “Worst” thermal conditions in both seasons. The results demonstrated that 1-m resolution maps offer substantially improved accuracy in capturing fine-scale spatial variations such as vegetation cover, building shadows, rooftops, and narrow alleys that are largely obscured in conventional 30-m Land Surface Temperature (LST) datasets. This high-resolution analysis revealed thermal heterogeneity within individual 30-m pixels, which challenged the reliability of coarse-resolution data for urban thermal assessments. Compared to previous studies using lower-resolution LST data, our findings highlight the potential for misinterpretation of surface temperature–urban form relationships when sub-pixel variability is not accounted for. Thus, the study underscores the importance of fine-resolution data for more accurate thermal pattern detection and for improving urban heat mitigation strategies. These findings suggest that enhancing spatial resolution of UST in heterogeneous urban areas can contribute to a better understanding of thermal distribution in cities, thereby providing more effective tools for environmental policymaking and improving urban living conditions.
