AB  - Accurate and computationally efficient classification of hyperspectral images (HSI) remains a challenging task due to spectral redundancy, limited labeled samples, and high data dimensionality. This study presents a regression-derived probabilistic feature generation framework for lightweight HSI classification. Unlike conventional methods that directly predict class labels, the proposed approach adopts a two-stage process. In the first stage, class-wise regression models are trained to map spectral responses to continuous estimates of class probability. The resulting probability maps are then concatenated into a compact, low-dimensional probabilistic feature space, which serves as input to standard classifiers in the second stage. Experiments on three benchmark datasets—Indian Pines, Pavia University, and Salinas—using three representative classifiers (SVM, RF, and MLP) under multiple train/test ratios (70/30, 60/40, and 50/50) demonstrate consistent performance gains across all metrics. The proposed framework improved Overall Accuracy by approximately 4%, Average Accuracy by 6%, F1-score by 5%, and Kappa coefficient by 4% compared with direct classification. Statistical analyses using paired t-tests and Wilcoxon signed-rank tests confirmed that these improvements are highly significant (p < 0.002). Moreover, the regression-based framework outperformed the lightweight 3D-CNN model across all three datasets (by approximately 0.3–2.0% points) and surpassed the 2D-CNN baseline in two datasets, trailing by only ∼1.1% points on the third. Overall, this study demonstrates that regression-derived probabilistic representations can substantially enhance class separability, generalization, and efficiency, providing an interpretable and resource-aware alternative for hyperspectral image classification.
ER  -

