AB  - Forecasting stock indices remains challenging due to nonlinearity, nonstationarity, and multiscale dynamics in financial time series. This study proposes the CEEMDAN-SE-VMD-BiGRU-Ridge framework, a novel hybrid model that integrates hierarchical decomposition and adaptive prediction to improve accuracy. The method applies complete ensemble empirical mode decomposition with adaptive noise (CEEMDAN) for initial decomposition into intrinsic mode functions. Sample entropy and K-means clustering classify modes into high-frequency, medium-frequency, low-frequency, and residual groups. Variational mode decomposition (VMD) is then applied specifically to further decompose the high-frequency group, suppressing noise and achieving precise noise separation. Bidirectional gated recurrent units (BiGRU) model the high-frequency decomposed IMFs from VMD and the medium-frequency components, while ridge regression captures stable trends in the low-frequency and residual components. Final forecasts are obtained by adding all component predictions linearly. Out-of-sample evaluation on six major global indices—Financial Times Stock Exchange 100 (FTSE), Shanghai Stock Exchange Composite (SSEC), Standard & Poor’s 500 (S&P 500), Deutscher Aktienindex (DAX), New York Stock Exchange Composite (NYSE), and Hang Seng Index (HSI)—using 70-30 hold-out shows excellent performance. The framework achieves R2 values of 0.9958 (FTSE), 0.9944 (SSEC), 0.9994 (S&P 500), 0.9992 (DAX), 0.9986 (NYSE), and 0.9975 (HSI), with substantially lower root mean square error (RMSE), mean absolute error (MAE), symmetric mean absolute percentage error (SMAPE), and higher R2 than thirteen benchmark models. Modified Diebold-Mariano tests (M-DM) and Model Confidence Set (MCS) analysis confirm statistical superiority. Backtesting yields higher Sharpe ratios, with robustness validated across splits, three business cycles (pre-pandemic expansion, COVID-19 recession, post-crisis recovery), and two contrasting market regimes (COVID-19 stimulus bullish phase, Russia-Ukraine conflict bearish period) as well as varying temporal lookback window. These results highlight the effectiveness of hierarchical decomposition and adaptive deep learning for precise financial forecasting and investment applications.
