AB  - Wetlands are among global biodiversity hotspots, providing critical habitats for rare species and serving as essential “blue carbon sinks”. Their preservation is vital for maintaining global ecological balance and promoting sustainable development. Quantifying vegetation diversity is fundamental to wetland conservation and restoration. While remote sensing technology has been utilized for this purpose, it still faces great challenges including inaccurate diversity estimation, and limited model generalizability, ambiguous relationships between remote sensing features and diversity metrics. To address these limitations, this study proposed a novel Deep learning-informed Interpretable framework for Remote sensing assessment of Vegetation species diversity (DIRV). DIRV integrates a Multi-Scale Spectral-Spatial Fusion Network (MSSFNet) with α- and β-diversity calculations derived from species-level classification, which achieved highly accurate translation of remote sensing images to vegetation species diversity mapping (R2 = 0.813–0.997). Results revealed MSSFNet accurately delineated the spatial distribution of 25 land cover types across heterogeneous wetland ecosystems (Overall Accuracy = 0.869–0.942). Compared with HRNetV2, DeepLabV3+, and Swin Transformer, MSSFNet consistently achieved the highest performance across three wetland scenes, outperforming the overall accuracy and Kappa coefficient of these mainstream models by 0.3%–12.7% and 0.2%–14.8%, respectively. We found that combining Spectrum and LiDAR data improved classification accuracy more significantly for karst wetland vegetation species than for mangrove forest. Furthermore, karst wetlands exhibited higher local species richness (α-diversity = 1.87) and pronounced compositional heterogeneity among patches (β-diversity: Bray-Curtis = 0.0–0.93; Jaccard: 0.16–1.00), whereas mangrove wetlands, though less species-rich (α-diversity = 1.74), displayed marked β-diversity gradients across tidal elevation zones (Bray-Curtis: 0.0–0.93; Jaccard: 0.18–1.00). In both wetland types, vegetation species diversity followed a unimodal pattern, initially increasing then declining with rising flooding frequency. Furthermore, RBRI, GRRI, GBDI and CanopyCover were key for estimating mangrove species diversity. The DIRV framework establishes a new paradigm for wetland vegetation diversity monitoring—shifting from mere pattern mapping to mechanistic understanding—and provides a scalable tool for safeguarding global wetland ecosystems in the face of environmental change.
ER  -

