AB  - Many studies were carried out to decide optimum Land-Use/Land-Cover classification algorithms focusing on assessing the difference between traditional approaches of LULC Classification. However, there have been less studies linking the performance of machine learning algorithms like Random Forest (RF), Correlation and Regression Tree (CART), Naïve Bayes (NB), Gradient Tree Boosting (GTB) and Support Vector Machine (SVM) especially on mining dominated landscape. There are no such studies in the Jharkhand-Odisha Region. Study is conducted mostly around the important iron ore, chromite and dolomite mining areas. This study investigated the performance of five machine learning algorithms (RF, CART, GTB, NB and SVM) using Landsat data in forest-mining dominated landscape to observe the impacts of mining on forest as well as other landscape dynamics. The results shows that GTB classifier outperformed other classifiers in such a complex landscape dominated by 70% of the forests and mixed agricultural practices. Overall accuracy showed by GTB is 0.890 in optical and 0.895 in fusion data in contrast to lowest accuracy posed by NB i.e., 0.767 in optical and 0.733 in fusion datasets. The performance of models was also evaluated by ANOVA and Tukey-HSD-test. Hence, GTB is recommended for the classification in similar landscapes.
