AB  - Joint ground observation of hyperspectral image (HSI) and light detection and ranging (LiDAR) remote sensing offers the possibility of obtaining more fine-grained feature interpretation. However, practical applications often encounter a situation where one HSI and LiDAR region has only a few labeled samples, and sometimes none, whereas another HSI and LiDAR region with similar land cover classes has a sufficiently large number of labeled samples. This presents great challenges to traditional multimodal deep learning fusion classification. Therefore we attempt to classify an HSI scene with only a few samples with the help of a similar scene with a large sample size of both HSI and LiDAR, and propose a few-shot learning (FSL) framework based dual-source hierarchical frequency domain fusion for HSI and LiDAR classification (HiF2-FSLF). Unlike existing methods that either rely solely on HSI or perform simple feature-level fusion, HiF2-FSLF introduces a frequency-domain perspective to explicitly model the complementary information between HSI and LiDAR. Specifically, A multiscale collaborative block (MSCB) is proposed to extract shallow features of the dual-source data, and enhanced sensitivity to local information while enabling the coexistence of global large-scale information and local detailed information. Then, a feature intertwine invertible module (FIIM) is developed to capture high frequencies by adopting a step-by-step decomposition strategy. During the fusion phase, a hierarchical frequency fusion attention method (H2FAM) is designed to refine the effective features through the three-branch attentions to realize the complementary fusion between HSI and LiDAR. After extensive experiments on the publicly four datasets with only five training samples per class, the classification performance of the proposed method outperforms other state-of-the-art methods, reaching 97.83 % OA in Houston2013, 94.57 % OA in Trento, 85.20 % OA in MUUFL, 88.33 % in Augsburg.
