AB  - Change detection (CD) is a critical interpretive application in remote sensing (RS) that aims to investigate variations in land cover from bi-temporal RS images, and serves in the fields of environmental monitoring and urban planning. Current CNN-based CD methods struggle to capture global features, leading to insufficient detail detection in change regions. While the long-range dependencies of Transformer-based and Mamba-based models aid in detail expression, they still have shortcomings in effectively detecting false changes. Alternatively, with the iterative generation and multi-step reasoning processes, diffusion-model-based CD methods exhibit strength by progressively refining discrepancies between bi-temporal RS images. In this paper, we propose a novel discrete diffusion-based CD paradigm and establish a state-driven discrete diffusion model with cohesive attention embedding (SDDCA) for change region generation. Due to the inherent characteristics of the CD task, we particularly define the two discrete states denoted as changed and unchanged to reflect the variations in the transition matrices within the SDDCA model. Notably, existing diffusion-model-based CD methods count on the addition and estimation of Gaussian noise to realize the diffusion process, which involves enormous diffusion iteration steps and model parameters. By contrast, with the presented binary diffusion pattern, the proposed model facilitates the generation of high-quality change masks and offers greater efficiency than traditional diffusion-model-based CD methods. Furthermore, we present the cohesive attention embedding (CAE) module that seamlessly integrates discrepancy features into the discrete diffusion process through a hybrid cross-attention mechanism. Additionally, the presented multi-domain discrepancy (MDD) module and the pyramid hierarchical fusion (PHF) module acquire and concatenate frequency information with spatial features for the CAE module. Comprehensive experiments on three publicly accessible CD datasets demonstrate that the proposed SDDCA outperforms other state-of-the-art (SOTA) methods, and achieves an F1-score of 91.24%, 92.96%, and 87.20% on LEVIR-CD, WHU-CD, and GZ-CD, respectively.
