AB  - Tensors as multidimensional data representations have garnered significant research attention in machine learning. While recent advances in support tensor machines using tensor train (TT) and tensor ring (TR) decompositions have shown promising results, they are limited to modeling interactions only between adjacent modes and exhibit sensitivity to tensor mode ordering, which restricts their expressive power and robustness. To overcome these fundamental limitations, we propose a novel support fully-connected tensor networks machine (FCTN-STM). At its core, FCTN-STM employs a fully-connected tensor network (FCTN) decomposition to model the weight tensor, thereby capturing intrinsic correlations between any two modes of the tensor, which leads to stronger model expressiveness and structural flexibility. Furthermore, to enhance computational efficiency without compromising accuracy, we introduce a safe screening rule (SSR-FCTN-STM) and integrate it into an alternating projection solution framework. This rule reduces each projection sub-problem by identifying and removing redundant variables during training. Its key advantage is safety, i.e., it guarantees that the removed variables are indeed redundant, ensuring the solution remains identical to the original problem. Experiments on seven real-world tensor datasets, including face (ORL), hyperspectral (HSI), functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI), and electroencephalogram (EEG) signal datasets, demonstrate that: 1) our SSR-FCTN-STM significantly outperforms existing methods in classification accuracy; 2) the SSR mechanism substantially reduces training time while fully preserving model accuracy. This work provides a novel and practical solution for efficient and powerful tensor learning.
