AB  - Rapid Land Use and Land Cover (LULC) changes driven by agricultural intensification, urban expansion, and river dynamics pose challenges to sustainable land management. Most studies lack long-term LULC analyses in erosion-prone deltaic environments, such as Shibchar, Bangladesh, and commonly rely on a single classifier with limited change metrics. This study addresses these gaps through a multi-decadal (2002–2022) assessment using ensemble machine learning and a change framework incorporating gain, loss, stable, and no-occurrence dynamics. LULC maps were derived from Landsat 7 (30 m) and Sentinel-2 (10 m) imagery using an ensemble machine learning approach in Google Earth Engine. A total of 2,106 reference samples (2002) and 1,975 (2022) were used with a 70/30 training–validation split. LULC change analysis was conducted using the MOLUSCE plugin in QGIS and conditional change detection in ArcGIS. The classification achieved >91% overall accuracy with a Kappa of 0.88. Results show declines in cropland (28 km2) and forest (9 km2), while waterbodies and built-up areas increased by 21 km2 and 15 km2, respectively. Transition analysis indicated strong conversion from built-up areas (53%), fallow land (38%), cropland (17%), and forest (9%) to waterbodies. Conditional analysis reveals forest (43.35 km2) and cropland (64.03 km2) with moderate stability but substantial losses, while waterbodies (36.10 km2) and built-up areas (14.75 km2) expanded. Fallow land remains highly unstable, reflecting rapid turnover. These findings support evidence-based land-use planning by identifying stable, vulnerable, and transition-prone areas, providing a basis for sustainable agricultural management, urban development, and ecosystem conservation.
