AB  - Transformer-based models and Convolutional neural networks (CNNs) have been widely used for hyperspectral image classification (HSIC) due to their ability to extract global and local features; however, transformer-based models are not well-suited for fine-grained local spatial patterns and incur high computational cost, whereas CNNs struggle to model global context and long-range dependencies. Recently, Retentive Networks (RetNet) have been proposed and shown good performance in HSIC with low inference cost and good training parallelism; however, RetNet still struggles to capture local information. To address these challenges, this study proposes a Depth-wise Separable Convolution-based Swin Retentive Network (DSCSRN) for HSIC, which consists of three modules: 1) a depth-wise separable 3D convolution with a squeeze-and-excitation (DS3DC-SE) module to capture rich local spectral–spatial relationships at low computational cost, 2) a data augmentation module to enhance the model’s learning capacity and robustness, and 3) a Swin Retentive Network (SRN) module using a shifted-window mechanism to model hierarchical and complex global spectral-spatial structures and long-term dependencies efficiently. Together, these modules enable DSCSRN to learn intrinsic spectral-spatial relationships from both local and global perspectives while maintaining a relatively low computational cost. Furthermore, tensor singular spectrum analysis (TSSA) is used to denoise the data and extract robust, low-rank, and representative features, which further improve classification performance. Experiments on four widely used HSI datasets, i.e., Indian Pines, Pavia University, Salinas, and HyRANK-Loukia, demonstrate that DSCSRN achieves OAs of 99.47%, 99.92%, 99.98%, and 93.25%, respectively, outperforms state-of-the-art methods, and provides a competitive and parameter-efficient solution for HSIC.
