AB  - Cold region paddy soil salinization has very strong spatio-temporal difference, single source observation is hard to control. This paper reinterprets salinity monitoring from being an A I driven spatiotemporal learning/decision support exercise and builds out the entirety of this computational pipeline starting off at a plot - time data cube combining satellite surf. reflectance (Sentinel - 2 /Landsat), in - sit soil moisture & bulk elect. conductivity sensor denoted as), auto. weath. station and plots manage yield records. Pipeline has two linked AI models, (i) An attention based temporal multitasking network for phenology consistent growth monitoring and early warning of saltiness str es s which is made for supporting farms' sever/edige inference by controlling depth, hidden dimension and attention heads; (ii) Supervised salinity inversion module which maps multi-source data features to plat - /pixel-scale laboratory saturation paste extract conductitivity (ECe) for salinity class mapping and downstream risk assessment. Time+space independence under the split with strict time + spatial block to eliminate temporal leakage and spatial autocorrelation, Temporal DL is optimal inversion model with RMSE 1.45 dS/m, R2 0.83, and salinity class discrimination AUC 0.92. For making AI outputs actionable and auditable, research integrates model explainability (grouped permutation importance/SHAP) and uses minimal predictive uncertainty estimation through Monte-Carlo dropout for deployment time confidence tagging. Regions then also display a higher-risk pattern (West has it, East gets less) with more irrigation district-level alerts of yield risk to address. Proposed the AI-powered frame which closes sense, study loop and choice circle in great scale of digital agriculture keeping watch on paddy fields in cold regions.
