AB  - Bi-temporal change detection task has an important application value in the field of computer vision, and is widely used in many fields such as disaster monitoring, urban extension analysis, and ecological change tracking. Currently, researchers extensively use deep learning models for change detection. In particular, architectures based on Transformer and CNN excel in extracting local features. However, these models struggle to handle long-sequence dependencies and capture fine details, especially in complex change detection (CD) tasks. Moreover, current change detection methods are limited by the size of available datasets, which makes it difficult to learn complex local changes effectively. The high cost of getting labeled data for high-resolution remote sensing images further complicates this problem. Additionally, existing data augmentation techniques do not create enough variety for effective representation learning, which significantly limits the ability to generalize well. To address these challenges, we propose a extensive self-supervised learning approach, which significantly expands the scale and diversity of self-supervised learning. A visual super-alignment strategy is introduced to align different hardness samples, which utilizes the hardness degree among coarse data to guide a fine-grained super-alignment training. By this means, state-space models are allowed to activate more neurons and capture complex patterns. Extensive experiments show that the proposed method demonstrates superior performance across multiple datasets, significantly enhancing the effectiveness of change detection tasks. Our code is available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/SAMAMBA-BEAE.
