AB  - In hyperspectral image incremental classification, few-shot class-incremental learning methods require multiple trainings during the incremental stage to adapt to new tasks. This makes the practical deployment of hyperspectral image incremental classification methods in real-world applications time-consuming and challenging to adapt to dynamically changing environments. Although few-shot class-incremental learning enable models to adapt to new-class while retaining old knowledge, limited labeled samples can easily lead to feature representation bias, resulting in estimation bias of classification results and exacerbating catastrophic forgetting. To address these challenges, we propose a training-free PSCEN. During the incremental stage, PSCEN does not require additional training to update the model and can be deployed with minimal resources, making it especially advantageous for real-world hyperspectral imaging applications. Furthermore, PSCEN prevents overfitting on few-shot samples and maximizes the retention of old knowledge. PSCEN mainly improves classification performance by enhancing the prototype representations of base-class and new-class. Specifically, we develop a prototype global similarity enhancement mechanism to adaptively enhance the robust representation of the base-class prototype by using the global central prototype. Additionally, the representation bias of new-class will lead to misclassification of new-class. Therefore, we propose a prototype similarity constraint calibration mechanism to improve the adaptability of new-class by constructing similarity constraint prototype to calibrate the representation bias of new-class prototypes. Numerous experiments conducted on Indian Pines, Kennedy Space Center, and Salinas have demonstrated that PSCEN offers superior efficiency and accuracy, showcasing excellent overall performance.
