AB  - Lakes are crucial indicators and regulators of climate change, moderators of regional climate dynamics, and pointers of global environmental shifts. However, the underlying meteorological and hydrological mechanisms shaping lake water levels remain inadequately understood. This study leveraged ICESat-2 satellite data to measure the water level of 237 medium and large lakes across China’s five lake regions in 2019‒2024. Four machine learning models were employed to fill missing water-level data, facilitating a detailed examination of the spatiotemporal dynamics in seasonal variations of lake water level (LWLsv) and lake water storage (LWSsv). Sixteen feature climatic, hydrological and surface-physical variables were selected for modeling. Correlation and trend analyses assessed the impact of drought factors, as measured by meteorological and hydrological drought indices, on LWSsv changes under compound conditions. The findings reveal that: (1) ICESat-2 satellites provide effective monitoring of LWL changes despite data gaps for some lakes due to size and shape limitations; (2) Among the tested models, XGBoost demonstrated superior performance, highlighting significant spatial variability in LWLsv across China’s lakes, discovering a macro-scale nationwide pattern of “higher levels in the east, lower levels in the west, stable in plateaus, and large fluctuations in plains”; (3) From 2019 to 2024, the total LWSsv for Chinese lakes reached 53.80 Gt, mirroring considerable inter-regional spatial heterogeneity in LWLsv patterns. Lakes in the middle and lower Yangtze River accounted for approximately 50% of the national LWSsv, while the Qiangtang Plateau’s inland lakes ranked second due to their abundance and size; (4) Water storage fluctuations displayed distinct responses to meteorological, hydrological, and compound conditions, with around 140 lakes influenced jointly by climatic and hydrological factors. The dominant drivers showed notable regional variations, reflecting the intricate interactions between meteorological and hydrological processes in lake ecosystems. The findings offer key scientific insights for water hydrological management and biodiversity conservation.
