AB  - Hyperspectral Image (HSI) classification is a fundamental task in remote sensing but remains challenging due to high dimensionality, limited labeled samples, spectral redundancy, strong spatial heterogeneity, and model overfitting. To address these challenges, this paper proposes S2A-TripNet, a novel spectral–spatial triplet learning framework that integrates spectral-aware adaptive triplet mining with dual-level data augmentation for robust HSI classification. Unlike existing approaches, S2A-TripNet jointly exploits spectral- and patch-level embeddings and augmentations to learn highly discriminative and stable feature representations under limited supervision. The framework uses Incremental Principal Component Analysis (IPCA) to reduce spectral redundancy, followed by three-dimensional spectral–spatial patch extraction to capture local contextual information. To further enhance robustness and mitigate overfitting, complementary spectral- and patch-level MixUp and CutMix strategies are incorporated to enrich the diversity of the training set. A triplet embedding network guided by a spectral-aware adaptive miner enforces strong intra-class compactness and inter-class separability in the learned feature space. Extensive experiments on three benchmark datasets—Indian Pines (IP), Pavia University (PU), and Salinas (SA) demonstrate that S2A-TripNet consistently achieves overall accuracies exceeding 98–99%, outperforming classical classifiers, deep convolutional models, and recent state-of-the-art methods. These results indicate that the proposed framework offers improved accuracy, robustness, and interpretability, making it well-suited for practical remote sensing applications such as environmental monitoring, precision agriculture, and urban mapping.
