AB  - In the context of orbital spacecraft maintenance, estimating the pose of non-cooperative targets is essential for accurate control and capture operations. However, space-borne imagery is subject to significant interference, compounded by environmental complexities such as the Earth background, which challenges the accuracy and robustness of deep learning models. This work primarily addresses Earth background interference, a critical yet underexplored challenge in orbital pose estimation. To address this issue, we propose a non-optimal suppression strategy designed to filter out interfering features while preserving effective ones. By leveraging a masked self-attention mechanism and the adaptive suppression of non-optimal keypoints, our approach extracts reliable and pertinent features to mitigate interference in space imagery. Building on this strategy, we develop the MGKSNet model for estimating the pose of non-cooperative targets; this model encompasses object detection, keypoint localization, suboptimal keypoint suppression, and pose resolution. Experimental results on the SPEED + dataset demonstrate that our model achieves a 56% improvement in rotational accuracy (0.4092°) and an 82% improvement in translational accuracy (0.0107 m) compared to existing state-of-the-art models. These findings validate the superiority of our proposed method and affirm its capability to meet the precision requirements for on-orbit space missions.
