AB  - Accurate detection of tea heat stress (THS) using uncrewed aerial vehicle (UAV) multi-spectral remote sensing images combined with deep learning techniques is crucial for THS management. However, most existing multi-spectral image fusion detection networks lack robustness in complex scenes and perform poorly in small-target feature extraction, leading to low detection accuracy. To address these issues, a spatial-frequency two-stream network (SFTNet) is proposed. It employs spatial feature extraction (SFE) and frequency feature extraction (FFE) branches to extract high-resolution spatial details from RGB images and rich frequency-domain features from NIR images, respectively. A feature enhancement structure with multiple attention mechanisms and channel interaction was designed at the output end of the FFE branch to effectively aggregate shallow and deep semantic information from the hierarchically aggregated feature maps. For multi-scale feature fusion, a multi-spectral spatial-frequency feature fusion (MSFF) module is developed to achieve tight alignment and integration of RGB and NIR features. To improve small target detection, a multiscale cross-attention aggregation (MCA) module has been proposed. By extracting fine-grained horizontal and vertical features while incorporating a cross-fusion strategy, the MCA module enhances SFTNet's learning of small target THS features, thereby improving detection accuracy for small target THS. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed SFTNet outperforms state-of-the-art networks across 4 m, 6 m, and 8 m datasets, achieving mAP@50 improvements of 5.9%, 7.7%, and 4.6% respectively. Furthermore, when evaluated on terminal devices in a non-deployment testing setup, SFTNet achieves an inference speed of 15.3 ms per RGB-NIR image pair, suggesting high computational efficiency under these specific conditions.
