AB  - The high nutritional value of blueberry has driven production scale expansion, with efficient harvesting and precision management becoming key for industrial upgrading—blueberry ripeness grading is its core link. Mainstream fruit ripeness detection methods use ordinary or hyperspectral images as data foundations, relying on machine learning-based computer vision. However, they have two critical limitations: only focusing on data correlation while overlooking causality, failing to unearth the intrinsic links between fruit growth and ripeness and thus resulting in misjudgments in complex scenarios; and poor cost and deployment adaptability, relying on large-scale manual annotations and high equipment costs, which cannot meet low-cost portable field needs. To address these, this study takes YOLO11 as the baseline and proposes YOLO11-WBO, a semi-supervised lightweight model for blueberry ripeness grading. Structurally, it integrates three customized modules: C2PSA_BCA includes causal attention and blueberry-specific features to enhance key information representation; C3k2_WT adopts wavelet convolution for accurate multi-scale feature extraction; C3k2_OREPA uses “multi-branch optimization during training and single convolution deployment during inference” to improve feature efficiency. A semi-supervised learning framework reduces reliance on large annotated data. Experiments on a blueberry dataset show YOLO11-WBO outperforms the original YOLO11n by 3.3 % in Precision and 4.6 % in mAP@50:95, maintains 8.33 ms per-image inference speed, and cuts annotation effort by 63 %. This work provides a low-annotation, low-cost, high-robustness solution for blueberry ripeness grading, with significant practical value and broad promotion prospects.
