AB  - Accurate nutrient assessment is critical for sustainable citrus production. This study advances non-destructive nutrient diagnosis by integrating Vis/NIR hyperspectral imaging with machine learning to predict macro- and micronutrient concentrations in ’Clementina de Nules’ mandarin leaves in commercial crops along the season. Two approaches were compared: (1) analysing mean leaf reflectance spectra using several machine learning approaches, and (2) processing full hyperspectral images with convolutional neural networks (CNNs). Models were trained using the full spectral range and a subset of wavelengths selected via a novel expert committee-based Sequential Forward Selection (SFS) algorithm to mitigate the curse of dimensionality. The mean spectral approach with a multilayer perceptron (MLP) achieved superior performance (R2 > 0.60 for most nutrients, up to 0.85 for Fe and 0.77 for B), while CNNs excelled only with selected bands (e.g., R2 = 0.82 for Fe). Traditional vegetation indices performed poorly (R2 < 0.48), highlighting the limitations of conventional methods. The modified SFS algorithm enhanced model efficiency by reducing redundant spectral data, demonstrating the potential of tailored machine-learning architectures for spectral-based nutrient diagnostics. These findings advance precision agriculture by offering robust, scalable solutions for real-time nutrient monitoring, addressing computational challenges in high-dimensional data and paving the way for optimised fertilisation management in citrus crops.
