AB  - Objective Airborne LiDAR point clouds accurately capture three-dimensional geometry and spatial structures, while hyperspectral imagery reveals material composition through continuous spectral bands, both of which show significant complementarity in tasks such as urban fine modeling and ground object semantic segmentation. However, most existing methods rely on rasterization to convert point clouds into two-dimensional digital products, which significantly limits the utilization of the rich three-dimensional geometric information inherent in LiDAR point clouds. Given the limitations of rasterization in preserving the inherent geometric structure of point clouds, recent deep learning networks have been proposed for semantic segmentation of point clouds, focusing on leveraging the unique characteristics of 3D data. Although these methods have made significant progress in point cloud semantic segmentation, most of them still take geometric features as the core and lack consideration of spectral information of ground objects, resulting in limited classification results for vegetation or composite ground objects with significant spectral differences. Therefore, this paper proposes a novel semantic segmentation framework for airborne LiDAR (ALS) point clouds, integrating hyperspectral channel masking to mitigate spectral redundancy and an adaptive attention mechanism to dynamically prioritize discriminative features, enabling more accurate semantic classification in complex outdoor scenes. Methods The proposed method mainly consists of three components: point cloud and hyperspectral data fusion, an attention-enhanced RandLA-Net architecture, and a hyperspectral channel masking strategy. First, the spectral information of hyperspectral imagery was projected onto the LiDAR point cloud through spatial mapping, enabling a joint geometric-spectral representation. Specifically, the point cloud and hyperspectral data were unified within the same geographic coordinate system and converted into pixel coordinates, after which the spectral responses of the corresponding hyperspectral pixels were assigned to the 3D points to enrich the point cloud features. Second, the baseline RandLA-Net was enhanced by an adaptive channel attention mechanism. Global average pooling and global maximum pooling were applied to extract complementary channel-wise statistical information from hyperspectral point cloud features. Non-shared multilayer perceptrons were used to model channel dependencies for each pooling branch independently, and learnable coefficients were introduced to adaptively fuse the pooled features, thereby improving the feature representation capability of the network. Finally, a hyperspectral channel masking strategy was employed to suppress over-reliance on specific spectral bands. Both masked and unmasked hyperspectral point clouds were fed into the network during training, and the model was optimized using label supervision together with a consistency constraint on prediction differences, which enhanced robustness and improved semantic segmentation accuracy. Results and Discussions The proposed method was evaluated on the self-collected YS-HP dataset and the public DFC2018 dataset. Experimental results demonstrated that the proposed approach could effectively classify the majority of airborne LiDAR point cloud categories. However, categories with limited training samples remained challenging and might still be affected by neighboring dominant classes. Quantitative comparisons with existing methods indicated that the proposed method consistently outperformed the baseline model. Specifically, overall accuracies (OA) of 91.63% and 75.89% and mean intersection-over-union (mIoU) values of 73.00% and 45.07% were achieved on the YS-HP and DFC2018 datasets, respectively. Ablation experiments further verified the effectiveness of each proposed component. Incorporating hyperspectral information, the adaptive channel attention mechanism (ACAM), and the hyperspectral channel masking strategy into the baseline model improved mIoU by 19.01, 20.59, and 22.51 percentage points, respectively. In addition, experiments with different numbers of masked spectral channels demonstrated that the proposed channel masking strategy consistently enhanced overall classification performance. Conclusions This study presents a semantic segmentation method for airborne LiDAR point clouds that integrates hyperspectral channel masking with an adaptive attention mechanism. To address the limited discriminative capability of geometry-only point cloud representations in complex scenes, hyperspectral information is first incorporated into LiDAR point clouds through spatial-spectral alignment. An attention-enhanced RandLA-Net architecture is then employed to improve feature representation by adaptively modeling channel-wise dependencies. Furthermore, a hyperspectral channel masking strategy combined with a dual-branch consistency constraint is introduced to enhance model robustness and generalization. Experimental results on the YS-HP and DFC2018 datasets demonstrate that the proposed method effectively improves feature representation and robustness for fine-grained point cloud semantic segmentation, achieving superior performance compared with most existing methods.
