AB  - Remote sensing image classification is a core research direction at the intersection of computer vision and remote sensing technology. Key challenges stem from inherent limitations of existing methods: single-scale feature extraction struggles to balance local details and global information, multi-scale feature fusion is prone to information loss or redundancy, and self-Attention mechanisms suffer from an imbalance between local and global feature representation. To address these issues, this paper proposes a multi-scale remote sensing image classification algorithm integrating self-Attention and local attention. Built on the ViT framework, the algorithm constructs a dual-branch multi-scale attention feature extraction and fusion module: The self-Attention branch captures global features, while the CNN-based CBAM enhances local detail perception. Serialization and deserialization mechanisms align the data formats of the two branches, and a weighted element-wise summation strategy achieves multi-scale feature fusion, ultimately generating a feature representation that balances global semantics and local details. Additionally, the algorithm embeds a multi-scale feature extraction module to strengthen the spatial hierarchical information of remote sensing images and improve the capture of small-scale objects and boundary regions. Experimental results verify that the proposed method exhibits superior robustness and adaptability in handling remote sensing scenarios with significant scale variations and sparse features, effectively mitigating the inherent limitations of existing methods in multi-scale remote sensing image classification.
