AB  - Sea fog is a pervasive maritime hazard, responsible for the majority of low-visibility-related transportation accidents. Accurate detection over vast oceans remains challenging due to observational sparsity and the spectral ambiguity between fog and low clouds in passive satellite data. To overcome this, we present a novel, physically constrained machine-learning framework that synergizes active lidar, geostationary imagery, and surface observations for robust sea-fog detection. Our approach first establishes a high-fidelity dataset by deriving accurate fog labels from CALIOP lidar profiles, guided by marine boundary-layer thermodynamics from reanalysis. Validation demonstrates expectational performance, with the mode achieving an overall Probability of Detection (POD) of 92.9%. These labels then train a Light Gradient Boosting Machine (LightGBM) model using multi-spectral infrared data from the FY-4 A geostationary satellite. A final post-processing step, incorporating ICOADS surface reports, further refines spatial coherence. This elevates the seasonal POD above 88%, reaching over 99% in summer, while maintain a corresponding Probability of False Detection (POFD) of approximately 12%. This study validates the power of integrating active and passive satellite sensing through machine learning, offering a scalable and effective solution for real-time maritime visibility monitoring and early warning.
