AB  - Most of the classical and intelligent methods developed for anomaly detection in earthquake precursor time series try to find points with abnormal sudden changes in the studied time interval. According to different unproven hypotheses that have been proposed for the LAIC (Lithospheric Atmospheric Ionospheric Coupling) mechanism, there is a time lag between the observed anomalies in different layers of the lithosphere, atmosphere, and ionosphere. Therefore, discovering the starting points of abnormal trends in the time series of earthquake precursors can be very effective in better understanding the LAIC mechanism. The Sequential Mann–Kendall (SMK) test is a widely used non-parametric approach for detecting structural changes, trend shifts, and regime transitions in time series. Unlike classical anomaly detection methods that focus on point anomalies, the SMK test identifies anomalies caused by changes in the underlying trend structure. In this study, using the SMK method and analyzing time series of different earthquake precursors obtained from satellite data, including electron density obtained from measurements of the Swarm (Alpha, Bravo, Charlie) and CSES-01 satellites, GPS-TEC (Total Electron Content), and products available on the GEE (Google Earth Engine) platform, including surface temperature, water vapor, and ozone, in a time periods of 170 (Swarm and GEE data), 102 (CSES-01 data) and 109 (GPS-TEC data) days, around the time and location of Turkey (February 06, 2023) earthquake, an attempt has been made to detect the onset time of possible LAI anomalies. The results emphasize the use of classical and even intelligent methods effective in detecting the exact time at which a structural change occurs in earthquake precursor time series for a better understanding of the LAIC mechanism.
