TI  - Dynamic Spectral Gating and Multi⁃Scale Entropy for Hyperspectral Image Compression
ST  - 动态光谱门控和多尺度熵用于高光谱图像压缩
PY  - 2026
T2  - Zhongguo Jiguang/Chinese Journal of Lasers
VL  - 53
IS  - 5
C7  - 0509002
DO  - 10.3788/CJL250824
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033056275?origin=resultslist
M3  - Article
DB  - Scopus
N1  - EID: 2-s2.0-105033056275
N1  - Export Date: 05 October 2026
N1  - Cited By: 0
AB  - Objective Hyperspectral images (HSIs) provide dense, continuous spectral measurements that characterize the physical and chemical properties of ground objects with high precision. HSIs are widely used in fields such as environmental monitoring, precision agriculture, land-cover classification and mineral exploration due to their ability to support fine-grained material discrimination. However, the high spatial and spectral resolution leads to substantial data volumes, which present significant challenges for data storage, transmission and onboard or real-time processing. Many existing compression methods cannot effectively balance compression ratio and reconstruction accuracy, particularly in scenarios involving low bitrates, limited computational resources or task-specific requirements. These limitations hinder the large-scale deployment of HSI systems in practical applications. To address this issue, this study proposes an efficient compression framework that jointly considers spectral redundancy, spatial-spectral dependency modeling and low-complexity entropy estimation. The goal is to reduce data volume while maintaining both perceptual and quantitative fidelity in the reconstructed images. Methods This study introduces DSG-LME-Net, an end-to-end learning-based compression framework specifically designed for hyperspectral images. The proposed method integrates two key modules: the Dynamic Spectral Gating (DSG) network and the Linear-complexity Multi-scale Entropy (LME) model. The DSG-Net utilizes a gating function based on the Gumbel ‒ Softmax sampling method. It adaptively evaluates the relative importance of spectral bands and selects more informative channels by allocating more bits to those bands. This dynamic gating mechanism enhances spectral selectivity and reduces redundancy, allowing the model to focus on preserving meaningful spectral features. The LME module is composed of four parallel context modeling components. First, the Unevenly Grouped Spectral Context Module processes spectral features in groups based on entropy levels, which allows flexible handling of spectral information with varying degrees of complexity. Second, the Checkerboard Attention-based Local Spatial Context Module captures spatial texture patterns efficiently using a sparse attention mechanism. Third, the Intra-slice Global Context Module models long-range dependencies within spatial slices, while the fourth component, the Inter-slice Global Context Module, focuses on correlations across adjacent slices. These modules are jointly designed to maintain linear computational complexity and enable parallel processing during inference. To further enhance the model’s adaptability to spatial structure, Adaptive Rectangular Convolution (ARConv) is incorporated into the encoder and decoder stages. ARConv adjusts the kernel shape to match local spatial variations, improving the flexibility of spatial representation. The overall framework is trained using a rate-distortion optimization objective that balances the minimization of distortion with bit rate efficiency. In addition, to address the discriminative needs of hyperspectral compressed features for subsequent tasks, this study proposes a task-aware auxiliary supervision mechanism within the compression model. By incorporating a lightweight classification head and simultaneously minimizing reconstruction error, compression rate, and classification error, the latent features demonstrate enhanced discriminative ability for downstream tasks while adhering to compression limitations. Moreover, the study outlines a systematic lightweight model design, which includes the use of smaller convolutional kernels, a reduction in feature channels, and the implementation of a key-value caching mechanism. This approach effectively decreases the number of parameters and computational demands, thereby enhancing the efficiency of model deployment. To verify the model’s effectiveness and adaptability, this study constructs three variants based on DSG-LME-Net: the lightweight DSG-LME-Lite, classification-guided (CE-Guided) DSG-LME-Net (incorporating a classification-guided mechanism), and its lightweight version CE-Guided DSG-LME-Lite. Results and Discussions Extensive experiments were conducted on six widely used hyperspectral datasets: Botswana, Kennedy Space Center (KSC), Salinas, Pavia Centre, Pavia University, and Houston. To validate the effectiveness of the proposed models, DSG-LME-Lite, CE-Guided DSG-LME-Net, and CE-Guided DSG-LME-Lite were compared against eight baseline compression methods. These baselines include traditional transform-based algorithms such as JPEG2000, BPG, and PCA; RGB-oriented deep learning models such as Pan, CHENG, and Hyperprior; and hyperspectral-specific networks such as FHNeRF and Verdú. Performance was evaluated using four common metrics: peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR), multi-scale structural similarity index (MS-SSIM), mean spectral angle (MSA), and correlation coefficient (CC). Across all datasets and bitrates, DSG-LME-Lite, CE-Guided DSG-LME-Net, and CE-Guided DSG-LME-Lite consistently outperformed the compared methods in both quantitative and perceptual measures. Despite a slight decrease in reconstruction quality across all three variants, their overall performance remained at a high level. In low-bitrate scenarios, these models demonstrated significantly improved spectral fidelity, with an average PSNR improvement of approximately 2 dB. Ablation studies were conducted to verify the contribution of individual components. The removal of either the spectral gating mechanism or the uneven spectral grouping strategy led to noticeable performance degradation, indicating the importance of each module. Additionally, as illustrated in Table 2, the enhancements in lightweight design have yielded impressive outcomes. DSG-LME-Lite notably lowers model complexity by narrowing the channel width and reducing the number of convolution layers, while preserving the encoding framework. This results in a considerable reduction in both the number of parameters and the computational expenses. As demonstrated in Table 3, this confirms that the collaborative optimization approach suggested in this paper successfully improves task discrimination capability while maintaining the compression rate. Conclusions This work presents DSG-LME-Net, a hyperspectral image compression network that combines spectral importance modeling with efficient spatial-spectral context learning. The use of a dynamic spectral gating mechanism enables adaptive bit allocation, while the multi-branch entropy modeling strategy captures dependencies across various spatial and spectral levels without introducing high computational overhead. Additionally, to meet the discriminative demands of downstream tasks, this work introduces a task-aware auxiliary supervision mechanism into the compression model. By adding lightweight classification heads and jointly minimizing reconstruction error, compression rate, and classification error, the latent features under compression constraints are enhanced for better task discrimination. Experimental results on six datasets confirm that the proposed method achieves state-of-the-art compression performance in terms of both reconstruction accuracy and spectral integrity. Moreover, the architecture is designed with practical deployment in mind, offering a favorable trade-off between performance and computational cost. In future research, we plan to explore lightweight variants of the model for edge computing scenarios and task-oriented compression strategies that integrate classification or detection objectives into the training process. Such developments may allow the compressed representations to serve not only for reconstruction but also for downstream analysis tasks.
