AB  - The Loess Plateau has long suffered from erosion and ecological fragility. In the Hua Jialing region, dominated by artificial plantations, we assessed ecological environment quality and its evolution over the past 30 years using Landsat imagery on Google Earth Engine. Four ecological indicators greenness, moisture, dryness, and heat were integrated into the remote sensing ecological index (RSEI). To uncover driving mechanisms, we further applied an explainable machine learning framework that combines LightGBM with SHAP. Results showed a marked improvement in ecological environment quality, with mean RSEI increasing by ∼15%. Spatially, ecological environment quality declined from southwest to northeast, with Longxi County maintaining the highest levels, while the Hui Ning and Anding boundary emerged as a degradation hotspot. More than half of the region displayed persistent improvement, whereas about 20% showed potential shifts from positive to negative trends, highlighting localized vulnerabilities. High-altitude areas demonstrated stronger ecological stability, with consistently higher RSEI values. The LightGBM with SHAP framework revealed that precipitation, soil moisture, and vapour pressure deficit were the dominant factors, explaining most of the ecological variation. Precipitation and soil moisture exerted strong positive effects, while vapour pressure deficit consistently reduced ecological environment quality. Altitude showed a secondary but positive contribution. SHAP interaction analysis emphasized that the combined influence of precipitation and soil moisture outweighed their individual effects, underscoring the importance of water availability in ecological resilience. These findings demonstrate significant recovery of the Hua Jialing ecosystem under long-term restoration efforts, while also pinpointing vulnerable subregions and key environmental drivers, offering guidance for targeted ecological management.
