AB  - The Anzali Wetland, a vital ecosystem in northern Iran, is experiencing increasing ecological stress due to climatic anomalies. Traditional meteorological methods often struggle to capture complex, nonlinear climate patterns. Therefore, deep learning approaches are essential for more accurate anomaly detection. This study uses ground-based meteorological data and satellite remote sensing observations to identify and analyze anomalies from 2019 to 2023 and their ecological impacts. Autoencoder-LSTM, CNN-LSTM, and BiLSTM models were applied to detect anomalies using reconstruction errors. Among these models, CNN-LSTM outperformed the others, achieving the lowest Mean Squared Error (MSE) and the highest R2, demonstrating effective detection and generalization. Anomalous days, primarily in winter and transitional seasons, were linked to intense weather systems, such as cyclones and cold fronts. Environmental indices derived from satellite data—such as Chlorophyll-a (Chl-a), NDVI, LSWI, and groundwater levels—were used to assess wetland responses. The results revealed reduced water availability and poor vegetation health, as indicated by lower NDVI and NDPI values, alongside declining groundwater levels. Following 2021, a noticeable shift from wetland to drier land types became evident. Additionally, spikes in methane (CH4) and nitrogen dioxide (NO2) emissions highlighted the impacts of both climate variability and anthropogenic activity. This study underscores the strong link between climate anomalies and ecosystem stability. It emphasizes the need for integrated monitoring to mitigate the negative impacts of climate change. The proposed framework offers valuable insights into climate-ecosystem interactions, supporting more effective wetland conservation and sustainable land and water management.
