AB  - The accelerated urbanization process in cities throughout Vietnam has markedly augmented the urban heat island phenomenon; nonetheless, the enduring correlation between land use land cover (LULC) transformations and land surface temperature (LST) remains inadequately quantified. In this research, Landsat imagery spanning from 1995 to 2025 was integrated with a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) model to categorize LULC and a single-channel algorithm to retrieve LST in Hanoi and Ho Chi Minh City. The CNN model achieved consistent classification accuracy with Kappa coefficients varying between 0.80 and 0.82; and overall accuracy exceeding 84%. The findings revealed that the pace of construction land growth in Ho Chi Minh City was nearly triple that of Hanoi, with an average annual increase surpassing 1,400 ha, while Hanoi exhibited a slower yet progressively extensive expansion trend into suburban regions. Furthermore, LST in both cities demonstrated a consistent upward trajectory, with decadal warming rates ranging from approximately 0.31 to 0.35°C. Concurrently, the rise in minimum LST suggested that the warming phenomenon had extended into regions that had previously exhibited comparatively lower surface temperatures. To elucidate the continuity of the association between construction land expansion and LST, a Cellular Automata-Artificial Neural Network (CA–ANN) model was utilized to project through 2035, suggesting that construction land would exceed 60% of the geographical area in both cities, while a robust linear correlation between construction area and LST would persist, with R2 values of 0.88 in Hanoi and 0.92 in Ho Chi Minh City. These results underscore the predominant influence of horizontal urban expansion on the thermal characteristics of surfaces, thereby establishing essential scientific foundations for the incorporation of urban heat island mitigation strategies into sustainable urban development planning in the future.
