AB  - Remote sensing integrated with a geographic information system (GIS) has become an indispensable tool, offering vast amounts of data for monitoring land cover, vegetation, water resources, and mineral deposits. As the need for sustainable resource management grows, leveraging remote sensing data in commercial applications offers a promising approach to balancing economic development with environmental conservation. This study presents a methodology leveraging remote sensing data integrated with GIS for a hybrid multilayer perceptron-recurrent neural network (MLP-RNN) model to support sustainable resource management in commercial applications. Using satellite imagery and ancillary data, the approach incorporates key remote sensing indices such as Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI) and Soil-Adjusted Vegetation Index (SAVI), temporal trends, and spatial gradients to analyze and predict resource dynamics. The model architecture combines MLP for spatial feature learning and RNN for temporal sequence analysis, achieving robust predictions with a mean absolute error (MAE) of 0.15, root mean squared error (RMSE) of 0.20, and an R-squared value of 0.92 on validation datasets. Data preprocessing includes atmospheric correction, feature normalization, and temporal alignment, ensuring model inputs optimized for accuracy. Tested in real-world case studies, the hybrid MLP-RNN model provides reliable forecasts that can guide resource allocation, market analysis, and sustainability assessments in sectors such as agriculture, forestry, and water resource management.
