AB  - AbstractUrban village (UV) detection, which aims to identify informal settlements with distinctive village-like morphology embedded within urban fabrics, is fundamental for precision urban renewal and sustainable city management. Existing datasets and detection methods have made progress in semantic segmentation of UVs, but they predominantly emphasize single-temporal mapping and neglect cross-temporal change analysis, leaving the dynamic evolution of UVs insufficiently explored. To fill this gap, we introduce ZY-UV, the first large-scale, high-resolution multi-temporal benchmark dedicated to joint semantic segmentation and change detection of UVs. Constructed from 2.1 m ZiYuan-3 satellite imagery, ZY-UV covers six geographically diverse Chinese cities and provides carefully annotated multi-city UV boundaries and paired bi-temporal image sequences. This design enables systematic evaluation of both spatial semantics and temporal dynamics under heterogeneous urban contexts and varying imaging conditions. On top of this dataset, we propose MutualTemp, a multitask learning framework that harmonizes semantic segmentation and change detection through a shared Siamese encoder and mutually regulated temperature parameters. By dynamically modulating task-specific feature distributions and balancing gradient flows, MutualTemp effectively mitigates task interference, adapts to relative task difficulty, and improves cross-domain generalization. Together, ZY-UV and MutualTemp form a comprehensive platform for advancing urban village monitoring, providing not only a challenging benchmark for evaluating multi-task and domain adaptation strategies but also a practical foundation for future studies in large-scale urban dynamics, cross-city knowledge transfer, and robust geospatial intelligence in rapidly evolving urban environments.
