AB  - Mining expansion across Ghana’s Pra River Basin has resulted in rapid and heterogeneous landscape transformation, creating an increasing need for methods capable of anticipating land-cover transitions rather than merely documenting changes after they occur. However, short-term forecasting remains constrained by spatially inconsistent observations, limited integration of predictive uncertainty, and insufficiently interpretable modelling approaches. To address these challenges, this study develops a forward temporal modelling framework that integrates spatial panel alignment, spectral interaction analysis, and probabilistic machine learning for land-cover transition forecasting in mining-influenced zones of the basin. Grid aggregation of Landsat 8 imagery acquired between 2022 and 2024 yielded a spatially consistent panel comprising 2100 temporally matched grid cells. The spectral structure of the land-cover classes was subsequently examined using Pearson correlation matrices and Graphical Lasso partial-correlation networks, providing a basis for assessing the stability of spectral relationships across classes and observation periods. Building on this representation, a Random Forest classifier was used to predict subsequent land-cover states from spectral features and prior class information. Strict chronological validation, with training on the 2022–2023 transition and testing on the independent 2023–2024 transition, provided an assessment of forward forecasting performance under temporally separated conditions. The model achieved a mean prediction confidence of 0.892 for 2024, while entropy-based uncertainty analysis indicated low ambiguity for most predictions (mean entropy = 0.176), with only 1.8% of observations exceeding the high-uncertainty threshold. Shapley Additive Explanations analysis identified vegetation-sensitive indices and shortwave infrared bands as influential predictors, while spectral interaction analysis revealed persistent coupling between Band 6 and Band 7 in mining areas. Applying the model to the subsequent 2025 period resulted in a moderate increase in predictive entropy (mean = 0.218; high-uncertainty proportion = 3.61%), indicating greater uncertainty in the one-year-ahead forecast. The findings demonstrate that a spatially consistent temporal representation of land-cover observations can support reliable short-term transition forecasting while preserving information on prediction confidence and feature contributions. By coupling chronological validation with interpretable and uncertainty-aware predictions, the framework provides a more informative basis for distinguishing confidently forecast transitions from areas requiring further investigation. This capability strengthens the use of medium-resolution satellite observations for anticipatory land-cover assessment and offers a practical basis for prioritising environmental monitoring in mining-influenced tropical landscapes.
