AB  - Driven by climate change and urbanization, extreme rainfall events are becoming more frequent. Traditional experience-based drainage design methods lack global optimization and efficiency, making them increasingly unable to meet cost-effectiveness demands. This study introduces a novel framework comprising four key steps: (1) land-use identification and hydrological characterization via an enhanced DeepLabv3+ model applied to satellite imagery, facilitating subcatchment delineation and runoff coefficient estimation; (2) development of the dual-criterion impact factor system considering both construction and layout rationalities, along with a weight matrix to examine the influence of these factors on design schemes; (3) automated generation of network topology and capacity calculation using the improved shortest path algorithm within a directed weighted network; and (4) multi-objective optimization based on SWMM simulations under various rainfall scenarios to evaluate Expected Annual Damage (EAD) and construction costs, thereby deriving Pareto-optimal solutions. Results indicate that the segmentation model, enhanced by incorporating a prior knowledge mask, effectively identified major urban land-use types, particularly buildings, roads, and water. Prioritizing construction-related impacts guided the model toward design schemes with more favorable slopes and lower costs. Under comparable flood risk conditions, the optimized design achieved a 9.4% reduction in the maximum pipe diameter, a 21.9% decrease in the average burial depth in localized regions, and an approximate 25.0% reduction in total cost. Applications to the districts in Lanzhou and Guangzhou demonstrated the framework’s robust applicability across diverse urban environments. The proposed framework presents a systematic, data-driven approach to generating cost-effective urban drainage designs with reduced manual effort.
