AB  - Remote sensing change detection (RSCD) aims to identify and analyze surface changes by comparing remote sensing (RS) images captured at different time points. Achieving precise detection remains a significant challenge in all the involved evaluation measurements. To reduce missed and false detection in change regions, We propose AFSNet, a high-performance change detection model extracting features with mobile backbone networks through progressive aggregation and full-scale connection using full-scale deep supervision for enhanced feature learning. Given the computational efficiency and parameter economy of mobile backbones outperforming mainstream architectures, we introduce a residual swin transformer module (RSTM) in the backbone to effectively capture and integrate both global and local semantic information. We then design a dual residual module (DRM) to extract bitemporal difference features across multiple levels and a convolutional attention block (CAB) to adaptively reweight multi-scale encoder features for improved detection by integrating attention modules into a full-scale fusion framework. Two RS public datasets, BCDD and LEVIR-CD, are used to validate the proposed AFSNet, and the results show that the F1-score of AFSNet reaches 94.51 % and 91.68 % respectively. All code as well as detailed instructions are available athttps://github.com/tawneydaylily/AFSNet.
