AB  - Flood depth is a critical parameter for disaster relief and damage assessment, yet pixel-level ground-truth data remain scarce. Traditional approaches, including digital elevation model (DEM) based methods and hydrodynamic simulations, are constrained by data accuracy, cost, and limited generalization. This study proposes machine learning (ML) and deep learning (DL) frameworks for flood depth estimation using only open-source datasets. Surface Water and Ocean Topography (SWOT) satellite observations were used to construct a pixel-level flood depth reference dataset for model training. Multi-channel inputs include DEM, slope, aspect, SAR/optical-derived flood extent, and AlphaEarth data, with channel- and self- attention mechanisms enhancing feature representation. Results from two test datasets show that both ML and DL outperform the baselines. Ablation experiments further confirm the value of attention mechanisms and Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) in handling high-dimensional features. Validation with in situ measurements from the Haihe River Basin indicates that different flood extent extraction methods affect results, though differences are not statistically significant. The DL framework further demonstrates improved robustness to DEM uncertainties, reducing RMSE by 10–50 % across three test datasets. Overall, the results highlight the potential of combining SWOT with open-source data for flood depth mapping, while noting limitations related to SWOT event availability and DEM accuracy.
