AB  - Hyperspectral image (HSI) consists of tens to hundreds of spectral bands offering exceptionally higher spectral resolution. Among numerous processing methods, Machine Learning (ML) technique is used in classification of hyperspectral data. This process leverages both spectral and spatial information, as well as their fusion, to extract meaningful insights from the data. HSI captures information across hundreds of narrow, contiguous spectral bands, providing detailed spectral data for each pixel. This high spectral resolution allows for the precise discrimination of objects. Effective classification of hyperspectral images supports applications in agriculture, environmental monitoring, mineral exploration and urban planning among many other fields. In this work, an advanced optimization enabled Deep Learning (DL) approach is used for HSI Classification. The classification process begins with preprocessing of HSI to improve data quality. This step uses proposed Adaptive Minimum Noise Fraction (MNF) method, which combines the traditional MNF with Adaptive Component Selection. Following preprocessing, the feature extraction phase takes place, focusing on both spatial and spectral features. Spatial features are extracted using Gabor filters, and Spectral features are derived using statistical measures such as mean, variance, skewness, and kurtosis across spectral bands. In addition, Shannon entropy is applied to extract entropy-based features. Finally, HSI classification is performed using proposed Meerkat Optimization (MOA)- Three-Dimensional Convolutional Neural Network (3D CNN). Here, 3D CNN is trained using MOA, which helps to optimize the model’s performance for accurate classification. Moreover, extensive experiments on benchmark hyperspectral dataset, like Indian Pines were shown to assess the proposed method. Experimental results demonstrate that MOA-based 3D CNN outperformed traditional methods, achieving accuracy of 96.5%, TPR of 96.8%, Kappa of 96.2%, TNR of 95.1%, PPV of 95.8%, and an F1 Score of 96.3%.
