AB  - Optical remote sensing images (RSIs) are often subject to cloud contamination during acquisition, resulting in substantial quality degradation and severely constraining their practical applications. We performed wavelet decomposition on cloud-contaminated images and found that thick clouds are mainly concentrated in the low-frequency components, while thin clouds cause disturbances in the high-frequency components. Some methods currently attempt to perform cloud removal in the frequency domain. However, directional information as a key characteristic of the frequency domain is often neglected. To address this, we propose a Direction-Aware Frequency Network (DAF-Net), comprising a directional classifier and a Direction-Aware Frequency Module (DAFM). The classifier determines the dominant direction based on high-frequency features in a lightweight manner, and the DAFM adaptively integrates multi-directional components to suppress thin cloud interference. The refined high-frequency features are subsequently injected into the low-frequency components, thereby enhancing reconstruction with improved structural coherence and enriched textural detail. The thick cloud dataset typically consists of multi-temporal data, while the thin cloud dataset is usually mono-temporal. Experiments on the multi-temporal Sen2_MTC dataset as well as the mono-temporal NUAA-CRL8/9 and CUHK-CR datasets demonstrate that DAF-Net outperforms comparison representative methods across evaluation metrics, including PSNR, SSIM, LPIPS, and RMSE. Furthermore, when processing multi-temporal images, DAF-Net is four times faster than the excellent diffusion model (DiffCR). It effectively improves cloud removal performance and provides a new perspective for the reconstruction of RSIs. The source code of DAF-Net model will be accessible at https://github.com/JJH0923/DAF-Net.
