AB  - Remote sensing binary change detection facilitates large-scale land surface change analysis and supports risk prevention tasks. Current methodologies employ multi-source image fusion to address spatiotemporal inconsistencies. However, these approaches frequently exhibit bidirectional interference issues, leading to erroneous detections and the oversight of actual changes. To address these challenges, this paper proposes a dual-branch collaborative interaction binary change detection (DCIBCD) model, which employs bidirectional interactive fine-tuning to mitigate interference effects. The DCIBCD architecture incorporates two fundamental elements: a bidirectional fine-tuned Siamese feature extractor (BFSFE) and a multi-source dfference-sensitive feature fusion (MDFF) component. The BFSFE utilizes a bidirectional refinement process to enhance localized multi-scale features, successfully capturing 1D spatial relationships through its integrated a large-scale axis mixed attention (LSAMA) mechanism. The MDFF executes simultaneous cross-branch and single-branch feature refinement to improve change identification accuracy. Comprehensive evaluations show DCIBCD’s outstanding performance on four standard datasets, attaining F1-scores of 90.44 % (LEVIR-CD), 90.52 % (WHU-CD), 96.09 % (DSIFN-CD), and 94.23 % (CDD), validating its effectiveness in resisting interference in multi-source data interaction scenarios. The code of our paper is accessible athttps://github.com/AIBox-IMU/Computer-Vision/tree/main/DCIBCD.
