AB  - This study investigates a new semi-observational analog approach (AnOb) for diagnosing convection related parameters. The new developed approach inherits from traditional analog forecasting but leverages deep learning computer vision algorithms for assessing weather state's similarity. The system integrates high-resolution RGB convection satellite imagery and ERA5 reanalysis data to enhance the characterization of convective processes. Three pre-trained DL architectures—VGG16, Xception, and Inception-ResNetV2—are fine-tuned and employed to extract salient convective features from satellite imagery. The model was trained over a four-year period (2020−2023) for lead times of 12, 15, and 18 h, with 2024 reserved for testing, covering a study region spanning 40°N–20°N and 20°E–4°W. The set of parameters includes Convective Available Potential Energy (CAPE), Total Column Water Vapor (TCWV), Vertical Velocity (W600), and Lifted Index (LI). Hence for a zero‑lead-time t, the DL algorithms are trained to create feature vectors for current and all past images. The similarity between the feature vectors is computed using cosine distance and the dates of the most similar past satellite images are selected. The ERA5-Reanalysis of the best analogs mean is then used as diagnosis of the convection parameters. This method mitigates some limitations in conventional analog systems, such as being mostly a point-wise approaches. To assess the system performance a two steps verification process has been achieved. First the mean hourly bias over the full test period 2024 is computed, then the mean bias is evaluated over a sample of 100 events equally distributed between 50 extreme convection events and 50 no convection events of 2024. For both full test period and extreme convection sample, the system exhibits enhanced performances compared to climatology and persistence, with hourly mean biases spanning between −200 J/kg and 200 J/Kg for CAPE, 0 °C to 1 °C for LI, and − 0.3 Pa/s to 0.3 Pa/s for W at 600 hPa. To assess the capability of the system to detect convection, a fuzzy logic was implemented based on operational thresholds. The system displays a Probability of Detection (POD) of 0.6, a False Alarm Ratio (FAR) of 0.4, a Brier Score (BS) of 0.4, and a Continuous Ranked Probability Score (CRPS) of 0.4. These metrics indicate a promising potential to detect severe convective events. This innovative AnOb system offers a cost-effective, high-performance complementary tool to traditional forecasting models, with significant potential for operational meteorological applications.
