AB  - Hyperspectral image classification is a challenging problem, primarily due to the high dimensionality of the data and the presence of a large amount of redundant information in the spectral domain. We propose a modular and flexible framework to combine various spectral band selection methods with both traditional classifiers and deep networks, in an effort to improve classification accuracy and reduce computational overhead. This framework includes a lightweight 3D convolutional neural network, Hyper3DNetLite, and additionally uses a support vector machine (SVM) classifier to compare simple and more complex models. Five band selection methods Random Forest (RF), Principal Component Analysis (PCA), ANOVA Ftest, Mutual Information (MI), and Sequential Feature Selection (SFS) are evaluated in order to identify the most informative spectrum bands. The framework uses per-band normalization and builds spectral-spatial image cubes to ensure consistent and dependable feature learning. Experimental results on the Indian Pines and Kennedy Space Center datasets show that SFS consistently outperforms RF and PCA with overall classification accuracy of 98.34% and 99.90%, respectively. ANOVA and MI, on the other hand, perform comparably worse, particularly when used with the Indian Pines dataset. These results demonstrate the positive effects of combining band selection with spectral-spatial deep learning and validate SFS and RF as feasible techniques for hyperspectral classification.
