AB  - The emerging Kolmogorov-Arnold Network (KAN) is an efficient nonlinear mapping method that works through univariate function decomposition. This capacity helps mitigate challenges in hyperspectral image (HSI) classification, such as high dimensional data redundancy and difficulty in capturing intricate nonlinear features. However, KAN’s discrete gradient update mechanism limits its ability to characterize the continuous variation patterns of features and lacks explicit modeling of the dynamic spectral-spatial interplay. To address these limitations, we propose a novel framework, named Kolmogorov-Arnold Network with Neural Ordinary Differential Equations (HyperKANODE), which deeply integrates KAN with Neural Ordinary Differential Equations (Neural ODEs) to achieve both parameter efficiency and dynamic modeling capability. Specifically, HyperKANODE introduces a tri-branch spectral-spatial dynamic modeling module named TriKAN, which incorporates learnable one-dimensional B-spline basis functions along the spectral, spatial width, and spatial height dimensions. By adaptively adjusting the grid points, the model accurately captures spectral absorption peaks and spatial texture heterogeneity, surpassing the coarse modeling of high-dimensional features typically imposed by conventional linear weights. The core innovation lies in the proposed KANODE layer, which reformulates discrete KAN transformations as a Neural ODE-driven continuous dynamical system, where B-spline nonlinear mappings explicitly parameterize the ODE vector field to govern feature evolution trajectories. Taking advantage of Neural ODEs, the model continuously tracks feature variation trajectories, enabling efficient end-to-end training and enhancing generalization to complex spectral-spatial patterns. Experiments on five publicly available HSI datasets demonstrate that HyperKANODE outperforms mainstream methods by 0.02%-5.85% in overall accuracy. Ablation studies further demonstrate the superiority of the KANODE layer in modeling dynamic learning processes, highlighting the potential of continuous dynamic modeling in advancing HSI classification.
