AB  - Floods are some of the most destructive natural hazards, which are driven due to the complex interactions between hydrological, meteorological and land-surface processes, which increasingly are being influenced by climatic variability. Traditional flood prediction modeling is based on single source data and is not able to reflect the spatiotemporal characteristics and non-linear relationships of the flood events, which results in lower prediction accuracy and late warning. This research focuses on improving flood prediction using the integration of heterogeneous remote sensing imagery as well as meteorological observation using a multimodal data fusion approach. A deep learning-based architecture made up of convolution neural networks (for spatial data feature extraction from satellite data) and recurrent/network for temporal modelling of rainfall temperature humidity was used followed by feature-level fusion and supervised classification/regression. Experimental evaluation conducted on datasets of multi-year floods shows that the proposed model increases the prediction accuracy by about 10~18% and conventional machine learning baselines for a higher prediction precision, better recall, and the detection of the onset of the volcanic flood in advance. These results mean that multimodal fusion is a good way of capturing complementary spatial and temporal information to create a robust and scalable solution to accurately forecast flooding and to better prepare for disasters.
