AB  - Long-term monitoring of braided rivers is crucial for understanding hydroclimatic variability and managing water resources. However, the limited operational period of modern high-resolution sensors like Sentinel-2 hinders multi-decadal analyses, and integrating historical Landsat with contemporary observations is challenging due to sensor differences and validation constraints. To address these issues, we propose an integrated deep learning frameworkwith three innovations: (1) FloodMamba-Net, a lightweight hybrid Convolutional Neural Network-State Space Model (CNN-SSM) architecture, achieving efficient and accurate flood extent segmentation with a water-class Intersection-over-Union (IoU) of 0.754 on the WorldFloods benchmark; (2) a simulation process that transforms historical Landsat observations into Sentinel-2 equivalent images through super-resolution and spectral synthesis; and (3) a “task-equivalence” validation strategy prioritizing functional consistency of derived geomorphic products over pixel-level radiometric fidelity,confirming that the simulated data are a reliable proxy for morphological analysis (dry-season water IoU > 0.6 in 2019, the final year of our 2017–2019 overlap validation period). Applying to a 150 km reach of the Heilongjiang (Amur) River, the framework successfully reconstructed geomorphic evolution from 1985 to 2024. Our analysis reveals a long-term stabilization trend, with decreasing braiding intensity, and a more positionally stable, contracted active corridor in recent years, resulting in a unique “stabilized and simplified” channel morphology. The study also uncovers lagged responses of the system to extreme flood events. This research provides a scalable solution for multi-decadal braided river monitoring in data-scarce regions and introduces the “task-equivalence” paradigm as a practical, generalizable approach for function-driven geoscientific studies utilizing historical satellite archives.
