AB  - The global climate, human settlements, and ecosystems are all seriously threatened by wildfires, which is why precise and prompt prediction is a top research priority. Current methods for predicting wildfires mostly use single-modality data, like vegetation indices or weather parameters, which restricts our ability to understand space and time, particularly in dynamic climatic conditions, these methods lead to decreased accuracy and delayed detection. The multimodal data fusion framework for integrated wildfire prediction presented in this paper is based on deep learning and integrates a variety of data sources, including vegetation indices, satellite imagery, meteorological data, and topographical information. The suggested system uses Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks to record temporal dependencies from weather and environmental data and Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) to extract spatial features from multispectral satellite images. Spatial-temporal representations are integrated by a feature fusion layer to create probabilistic maps of wildfire risk. According to experimental results, the CNN-LSTM fusion model suggested performs noticeably better than conventional single-modality and shallow learning techniques, attaining an overall precision of 94.6% with fewer false alarms and improved early detection capabilities in a range of climatic zones. These results confirm the ability of the model to assist long-term mitigation plans, proactive wildfire management, and disaster preparedness.
