AB  - With the rapid progress of generative models, such as generative adversarial networks (GANs) and diffusion models, it has become feasible to readily synthesize forged satellite images with high visual fidelity. This development presents substantial challenges to the existing forgery detection methods for satellite images. Furthermore, most current satellite image detection models are plagued by inherent limitations, including a lack of sufficient ability to capture high-frequency forgery cues, inadequate integration of multi-spectral features, and weak generalization performance under complex scenes and cross-domain data distributions. To address these challenges, a novel Spatial-Frequency Collaborative Fusion Network (SF-CFNet) is put forward for the detection of AI-generated satellite images. Specifically, a frequency-specific dual-attention (FDA) mechanism is devised to enhance the discrimination of crucial forgery-related features. Meanwhile, an adaptive multi-scale feature fusion module integrates hierarchical information across diverse scales. Additionally, a DCT-guided dynamic threshold strategy is incorporated to enhance the robustness of forgery detection under different satellite image qualities. Extensive experiments are conducted on a self-constructed SMTD dataset containing 20,000 samples, including 10,000 forged satellite images generated by GANs and diffusion models, as well as the public DeepFake_GeoSat dataset. Experimental results demonstrate that SF-CFNet consistently outperforms state-of-the-art methods under the main validated experimental setting, achieving an accuracy of 98.95%, precision of 99.75%, recall of 98.17%, and an F1-score of 98.96%.
