AB  - Anthropogenic pressures increasingly threaten coastal wetlands across West Africa, yet systematic assessments of their degradation remain scarce. This study quantified spatiotemporal land use and land cover (LULC) dynamics within Ghana's Keta Lagoon Complex Ramsar Site (KLCRS) from 2015 to 2023, using dense time-series Sentinel-2 imagery, Random Forest machine learning, and the Google Earth Engine platform. Classification achieved high accuracy (Overall Accuracy >89%; Kappa >0.84) across all periods. Two critical findings emerged: natural vegetation declined by 122.05 km2 and flooded vegetation by 25.17 km2, driven primarily by rapid settlement expansion (+82.98 km2) and salt mining operations, which exhibited the highest proportional growth (131% increase). The simultaneous expansion of saltpan areas (17.48 km2, 131%) and contraction of farmland (18.6%) between 2020 and 2023 in the same areas reveals a spatial association that warrants further investigation. While this pattern is consistent with salinization impacts reported elsewhere, establishing causality would require field-based soil and water quality measurements not available in this study. These findings confirm the KLCRS's unsustainable trajectory and underscore the urgent need for evidence-based policy interventions, including strategic zoning, promotion of sustainable livelihoods, and integrated coastal zone management, to conserve this ecologically significant Ramsar site. The findings of the study are essential for monitoring threatened coastal wetlands, with potential applicability to similar systems across West Africa.
