AB  - Missing data caused by cloud cover, sensor limitations, and orbital gaps remain a fundamental challenge for many satellite-derived geophysical products, limiting their utility in environmental monitoring and climate research. To address this issue, we propose a physically informed deep learning framework, the Peripheral Feature-assisted Fourier-transform Convolutional Bidirectional LSTM (PFTCBiLSTM), for reconstructing spatiotemporally coherent fields from incomplete remote sensing imagery. The model combines Fourier-domain convolutional layers, which capture spatial frequency characteristics, with bidirectional recurrent units that represent nonlinear temporal dependencies, while auxiliary geophysical drivers provide physical context for air–sea interactions. Using geostationary satellite observations over the South China Sea as a test case, PFTCBiLSTM demonstrated substantial improvements over advanced baseline models. The final configuration reduced the mean absolute error (MAE) from 0.33 °C for FTC-LSTM to 0.21 °C and the root mean square error (RMSE) from 0.51 °C to 0.35 °C, while increasing the structural similarity index (SSIM) to 0.98 and maintaining R2 = 0.98. Independent validation against NOAA iQuam in situ observations confirmed robust performance across both deep-basin and coastal regimes, particularly in cloud-prone and dynamically complex regions. Sensitivity experiments, Shapley Additive Explanations (SHAP), and adversarial robustness tests using projected gradient descent (PGD) further highlighted the model’s stability and interpretability. Although demonstrated here for an oceanographic application, the proposed framework offers a transferable solution for remote sensing image reconstruction. Its design can be readily extended to other satellite-derived variables, including chlorophyll-a concentration, aerosol optical depth, and salinity, providing a versatile methodology for advancing spatiotemporal data recovery across diverse Earth observation domains.
