AB  - Deep learning has become a common approach for constructing change detection models that help extract semantic features from raw remote sensing images. However, relying solely on semantic information derived from imprecisely aligned images can lead to the entanglement of change-relevant and irrelevant observations, restricting the representation of differences between images and resulting in pseudo-changes. To address these issues, this study proposes an implicit calibration and difference-guided hierarchical perception network (CDHNet), which integrates spatiotemporal consistency modeling with difference representation learning to enhance the discriminability of change features. Unlike conventional feature extraction strategies, CDHNet introduces an implicit calibration and difference-guided augmentation (CDA) module that enhances spatial correspondence between bi-temporal images and labels through pixel-level self-calibration and further reinforces spatiotemporal consistency via inter-branch mutual alignment. Building on this calibration, multi-order feature differences are extracted to mitigate the degradation of change-relevant representations caused by the locality of convolutional operations. Furthermore, CDHNet designs a staged dual-domain interaction (SDI) module, which enables cross-attentional integration between calibrated semantic and hierarchical difference features, thereby achieving a balanced representation of background and changes while enhancing the diversity of feature extraction. In the decoder, an adaptive multi-scale perception (AMP) module is designed to progressively improve discriminative perception along both channel and spatial dimensions, maintaining spatial coherence to achieve multi-scale object discrimination and enable accurate identification of both local changes and structural transitions. Experiments on four public and two unregistered datasets demonstrate that CDHNet outperforms the benchmark methods, particularly in preserving spatial completeness under complex global variations and accurately identifying subtle regional changes. The code for this work will be available on GitHub.
