AB  - Monitoring water quality parameters (WQPs) in coastal inland waters remains challenging because pronounced spatiotemporal variability and complex pollutant sources limit the robustness of conventional retrieval methods. High-resolution Earth-observation satellites improve spatial detail for fine-scale monitoring, but their application over large-area is constrained by substantial computational demands. Moreover, in optically complex waters, spectral information alone is often insufficient, highlighting the need to incorporate geographic and environmental predictors into WQP retrieval models. In this study, we developed a Google Earth Engine (GEE)-based workflow to retrieve four management-relevant WQPs across China’s coastal inland waters: permanganate index (CODMn), total nitrogen (TN), total phosphorus (TP), and turbidity (TUB). A large matched dataset was constructed by linking Sentinel-2 remote sensing reflectance (Rrs) with observations from automatic monitoring-stations, as well as geographic and environmental covariates. Based on this dataset, lightweight multimodal machine-learning models were developed to integrate optical, spatial, and environmental predictors within a computationally efficient, scalable framework. The models showed good predictive performance, with validation R2 values of 0.759–0.827 and independent test R2 values of 0.664–0.734, indicating satisfactory accuracy and transferability. Application to 2024 imagery in China’s coastal inland waters revealed clear spatial gradients, with CODMn, TN, and TP generally higher in northern regions, particularly in estuarine and nearshore waters. Seasonal patterns showed summer–autumn maxima for CODMn, TP, and TUB, whereas TN peaked in winter–spring. The proposed GEE-based framework provides a practical and transferable approach for WQP monitoring and water-environment assessment.
