AB  - Mining poses significant socio-economic and environmental threats, including deforestation, habitat destruction, and water contamination. Traditional detection methods such as manual surveys and basic satellite analysis are often inadequate due to the vastness of affected areas and the covert nature of such activities. This research evaluates and compares the performance of four Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) architectures—ResNet-152, DenseNet-121, EfficientNet-B5, and VGGNet-19—in detecting Unregulated mining sites from satellite imagery using semantic segmentation. A high-resolution, annotated dataset from the Roboflow Universe platform was preprocessed through normalization, augmentation, and mask generation for compatibility with deep learning models. Models were fine-tuned via transfer learning with optimizers including Adam, SGD, RMSprop, and Adam. Performance evaluation on the test set revealed that DenseNet-121 achieved the highest classification accuracy (75.00%), followed by ResNet-152 (73.08%), VGGNet-19 (72.12%), and EfficientNet-B5 (70.19%). These findings highlight DenseNet-121 as the most effective backbone among the tested architectures for Unregulated mining detection in the given dataset. The results can guide the selection of optimal CNN models for environmental monitoring, law enforcement, and sustainable resource management. Future work will explore multimodal data fusion, advanced architectures such as Vision Transformers, and real-time edge deployment for enhanced detection capabilities.
