AB  - Mangroves are critical coastal ecosystems that provide shoreline stabilization, storm protection, and carbon sequestration. Yet their ecological functions have been increasingly degraded by invasive species, typhoons, and human activities. Accurate mapping of mangrove distribution is essential for effective conservation, but conventional remote sensing approaches rely heavily on large annotated datasets and often fail in data-scarce or spectrally complex conditions. To address this challenge, we develop MetaSpecFSNet, a meta-learning-based framework designed for few-shot mangrove detection. The model is first pretrained on hyperspectral annotated data from general remote sensing scenes and then fine-tuned using samples from five representative mangrove sites, thereby reducing dependence on extensive labeled datasets. Sentinel-2 imagery from mangrove reserves in Hainan and Guangxi, China, was used for evaluation. Experimental results demonstrate that MetaSpecFSNet consistently outperforms mainstream deep learning models, including Convolutional Neural Network (CNN), Deep Neural Network (DNN), and Transformers. Leveraging the few-shot meta-learning strategy, the model achieved an average classification accuracy above 90% and an F1 score exceeding 65%. These findings highlight the potential of meta-learning to enhance ecological monitoring in environments where data are limited and spectral domains are highly complex, and provide valuable insights for evidence-based mangrove conservation and coastal management.
