AB  - Recently, although fully-supervised salient object detection in optical remote sensing images (ORSI-SOD) has achieved remarkable performance improvements, the pixel-level annotations required by such supervision paradigm are extremely labor-intensive and resource-consuming. To alleviate this issue, weakly-supervised ORSI-SOD methods, especially those based on scribble annotations, have made considerable progress. Unfortunately, prevailing weakly-supervised ORSI-SOD methods lack explicit modeling of multi-directional features for salient objects, and neglect the effective utilization of edge information, particularly the cross-scale exchange of edge knowledge. To overcome these limitations, we present MNEFNet, a weakly-supervised salient object detection network based on multi-directional nesting and edge scan fusion. The model integrates two key modules: the Multi-directional Nested Mamba Module (MNMM) and the Cross-scale Edge Scanning Fusion Module (CESFM). MNMM adopts a bi-level nested architecture inspired by Vision Mamba, consisting of weight simulation generation and state equivalent update in the outer path, as well as explicit directional encoding and internal Mamba units in the inner path. It enables the network to estimate saliency by capturing and reasoning directional characteristics of objects without pixel-level supervision. As for the CESFM, its core objective is to enhance the localization of salient objects and refine their boundaries. This module mainly contains an edge information cache and a cross-scale Mamba fusion component. Through the joint operation of these two parts, the hidden edge cues at multiple levels and scales can be stored, mined, aggregated and utilized efficiently and accurately. Extensive experiments on benchmark datasets demonstrate that the proposed MNEFNet achieves state-of-the-art SOD performance.
ER  -

