AB  - Hyperspectral target detection (HTD), which aims to search for targets in a hyperspectral image (HSI) through the provided target spectra, has become a research hotspot recently. In the HTD community, low-rank and sparse representation (LRSR) models play a vital role. Among these models, two crucial challenges are particularly noteworthy. The first challenge is to construct representative and pure dictionaries (a.k.a., robust dictionaries) and thus improve the representation effect of the background and targets. In addition, how to use valuable prior information to further enhance the separation degree of the background and targets is the other challenge. To solve these challenges, an HTD method by combining robust dictionaries (RD) and constrained energy minimization (CEM) regularized LRSR model (RDCEM-LRSR), in this article, is proposed. To better represent the background and targets, a graph-regularized saliency detection (GRSD) algorithm customized for HTD, along with spectral information divergence (SID), is combined to develop robust dictionaries. Moreover, to guarantee that targets can be effectively separated from the background, a CEM regularization term is designed and exerted into the LRSR model. Experiments conducted on six HSIs demonstrate that RDCEM-LRSR yields superior performance when compared to the most advanced existing methods.
ER  -

