AB  - Wind field retrieval plays a critical role in weather forecasting, climate change analysis, and marine transportation. In the early stages of wind field retrieval research, data-driven deep learning approaches have attracted considerable attention. However, many existing methods neglect the value of multi-temporal information—particularly cloud motion data, which is extensively used in cloud motion wind (CMW) retrieval techniques. To better utilize this information, this study proposed CMW_SimVP, a deep learning model that integrates CMW retrieval techniques with SimVP, a simple yet efficient architecture for time series prediction. The CMW_SimVP model extracts and synthesizes cloud motion features from three consecutive satellite images to improve the accuracy of 10-m wind field retrieval. Experimental validation using Level 1 Himawari-8 satellite data demonstrated that compared to models without cloud motion integration, CMW_SimVP improves wind field retrieval accuracy by more than 15%. Moreover, by leveraging the high spatial coverage and temporal resolution of geostationary satellites, our method achieves full-area coverage at fine temporal intervals. Unlike the numerical weather prediction models that require substantial supercomputing resources to generate wind fields, our method enables real-time retrieval of 10-m wind fields with a 10-min temporal resolution and 0.25° spatial resolution over the range of 0°–60°N and 100°–160°E within less than 1 s, using a single graphics processing unit and with mean absolute error rates of 1.0 m/s for both the u-component and V-component.
