AB  - Cross-modal image-text retrieval is a foundational technology in remote sensing (RS), enabling intelligent mining and semantic interpretation of large-scale earth observation data. Most existing approaches function as holistic matchers, projecting entire images and their corresponding sentences into a shared global embedding space to compute similarity. However, these methods achieve only macroscopic alignment and fail to identify the spatial grounding of specific textual concepts, thereby limiting further improvements in retrieval models. To address this limitation, a novel synergistic learning paradigm, SLB-Net, is proposed that explicitly incorporates fine-grained visual grounding to improve the precision and interpretability of RS cross-modal retrieval. SLB-Net introduces three novel modules: Optimal Transport-guided Query Selection (OTQS), Spatial Anchoring for Cue Recovery (SACR), and Cross-modal Retrieval via Task-adaptive Balancing (CRTB). The OTQS module applies a cost-minimizing transport plan with strict marginal constraints to extract precise spatial queries, preventing key regions from dominating and enabling the identification of subtle small objects. The SACR module employs a lightweight cross-modality decoder to generate explicitly localized bounding boxes from textual descriptions, providing essential geometric priors for subsequent alignment. The CRTB module, with a complementary balancing structure, integrates bottom-up spatial synergy and top-down semantic filtering to resolve feature conflicts between macroscopic retrieval and microscopic grounding. We evaluate SLB-Net on two RS image-text retrieval benchmarks. In addition to the original labels, we extend the benchmarks with additional region-text annotations. Extensive experiments across these datasets show that SLB-Net significantly improves overall retrieval performance over recent state-of-the-art methods (e.g., EAPA and SWPE), yielding an absolute improvement of 1.67% in mean Recall on the RSITMD dataset, while also providing transparent, fine-grained spatial evidence for cross-modal matching. The source code is available at: https://github.com/miaomiao101811-ui/SLB-Net/tree/main.
