AB  - Dissolved oxygen (DO) serves as a fundamental parameter for assessing the ecological well-being of coastal waters and is vital for sustaining the equilibrium of aquatic ecosystems. However, the trends and drivers of DO in coastal waters influenced by climate change and extensive human activities are still not well understood across vast spatial and temporal extents. Using the Greater Bay Area (GBA) as a representative urbanized coastal region, this study developed a robust ensemble machine learning (EML) algorithm to estimate DO concentrations and reconstruct a continuous DO time series from 1986 to 2024 based on Landsat satellite data. Thus, the reconstructed DO time series in the GBA were correlated with land-use and climate and factors to explore the underlying drivers of DO fluctuations. The results demonstrated that EML reliably estimated DO from Landsat Rrs, with Error, Bias, Slope, and mean absolute error of 8.31%, −0.1%, 0.30 and 0.75 mg L−1 on validation set, respectively. The DO concentrations remained relatively stable overall with an average of 6.04 ± 0.51 mg L−1, while hypoxic areas declined significantly from 1,445.2 km2 in 1986 to 271.4 km2 in 2024. The lower DO concentrations occurred in summer, with persistent hypoxia in estuarine nearshore zones. Nevertheless, historical trends indicated significant increases in DO concentrations and reductions in hypoxic extent. The SHAP-driven factor analysis indicated that these improvements were linked to land-use changes, including a reduction in cropland and urban expansion. This suggests that targeted policy interventions have mitigated the adverse effects of urbanization on coastal oxygen dynamics. These findings not only illustrate the applicability of remote sensing for reconstructing spatiotemporal DO patterns, but also offer valuable insights into the complex mechanisms underlying DO variability in coastal environments.
