AB  - Sentinel-1 wave mode (WV) synthetic aperture radar (SAR) routinely acquires high-resolution vignettes over the Southern Ocean, but its potential for automated and statistically oriented iceberg study has been underutilized. This work presents IByolo, a deep learning model adapted from the YOLOv11 architecture, for the detection and localization of km-sized icebergs in open ocean WV SAR data. The model was trained on a manually curated dataset of 13,686 annotated icebergs across 2062 vignettes. Sensitivity experiments over multiple architectural configurations identified the HGNetV2+C2PSA-CAA+P2 combination as optimal with AP50:95 of 0.41. IByolo generates reliable bounding boxes that enable efficient surface area estimations for icebergs within 0.1-5 km2. Application to 19,025 vignettes acquired south of 40∘[jls-end-space/]S during 2016–2018 yields 80,245 iceberg detections. Most vignettes contain fewer than ten icebergs with total surface areas below 5 km2. Icebergs are concentrated near major calving fronts in the northeastern Weddell Sea, Amundsen–Ross Seas, and coastal East Antarctica. Their population shows a pronounced seasonal cycle, with maxima in the austral summer and near-zero values in winter. This aligns with the Altimeter-based analysis for km-sized icebergs, and together they complement global observational records for further understanding of the Antarctic iceberg drift and changes. The retrieved size distribution follows a power-law scaling with an exponent of –1.42, which is consistent with prior satellite-based estimates. These findings confirm the viability of combining WV SAR data with advanced object detection frameworks for scalable iceberg monitoring. The resulting dataset would be a valuable resource for investigating Antarctic iceberg dynamics, freshwater fluxes, and their broader climatic impacts.
