AB  - The collaborative classification technology of hyperspectral image (HSI) and Light Detection and Ranging (LiDAR) data is increasingly becoming a research focus in the field of remote sensing, aiming to improve classification accuracy by integrating spectral and elevation information. Despite advancements in the classification of multimodal data, current methods suffer from certain defects. Some methods fail to fully exploit the complementary information between different data sources during feature extraction, while others overly emphasize the characteristics of a particular data source during the feature interaction stage. Furthermore, although some methods employ a cross-attention mechanism to integrate multimodal features, this approach often fails to appropriately weigh the features of different modalities, thereby easily neglecting the details within each modality's internal features and resulting in information loss. In view of this, this paper proposes a Transformer-based Cross-modal Difference Interaction Collaborative Frequency-domain Fusion Transformer Network (C2F2T-Net). Specifically, a simple two-branch Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) is first used for preliminary feature extraction. Then, to enhance the complementary benefits between HSI and LiDAR data and ensure that the intrinsic features of each modality are adequately emphasized, a Self-Cross Guided Attention Transformer is designed. This module aims to deeply fuse and refine the complementary information between the two data sources while meticulously preserving and enhancing the unique features of each modality. Finally, to deeply explore the complementary advantages between HSI and LiDAR data, a Frequency Domain Semantic Compensation Fusion module is introduced. This module facilitates semantic information exchange between the two modalities, enabling a mutually compensatory learning mechanism for more effective information fusion. Experimental validation was conducted on four standard HSI and LiDAR datasets, and the results demonstrate that the proposed C2F2T-Net exhibits superior performance in classification tasks.
