AB  - Accurate assessment of water quality parameters is vital for sustainable water resource management, particularly in irrigation-dependent regions such as the Middle Awash Basin, Ethiopia. Monitoring water quality is challenging due to its complex variability and the limitations of traditional in-situ methods, which are costly, time-consuming, and unable to capture real-time spatial and temporal changes over large areas. This study integrates Sentinel-2 multispectral satellite data with a Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (Bi-LSTM) deep learning model to estimate key water quality indicators, including, Total Dissolved Solids (TDS), Electrical Conductivity (EC), and pH. Utilizing in situ ground-truth observations and spectral reflectance data, the Bi-LSTM model achieved high accuracy, with coefficients of determination (R2) of 0.98, 0.97 for TDS and EC and 0.95 for pH, outperforming other machine learning approaches SVR, XGBoost and Random Forest Regressor. Temporal anomaly detection using Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) highlighted seasonal variability in water quality, influenced by hydrological and climatic factors, with anomalies predominantly occurring during the dry season. Spatial distribution maps derived from Sentinel-2 data visualized the concentration gradients of water quality indicators across diverse water sources, revealing critical hotspots like Beseka Lake and Deho Springs, which exceed permissible irrigation thresholds standards. The proposed framework demonstrates a scalable, cost-effective approach to continue water quality monitoring that overcomes the constraints of conventional in situ approaches. This study, which combines advanced remote sensing, deep learning, and cloud computing, provides critical insights into spatiotemporal water quality dynamics and presents a robust tool for water managers, policymakers, and farmers to optimize irrigation practices and mitigate salinity-related challenges.
