AB  - AbstractRemote sensing provides indispensable observations for ocean monitoring, and fully exploiting these data for temperature-salinity (T-S) research is scientifically crucial. Most studies focus on predicting sea-surface variables or inverting subsurface properties, yet oceanic energy transfer and material exchange are governed by the dynamics of the full three-dimensional (3D) field. To bridge this gap, we introduce 3D-OceanNet, a deep learning framework that infers the evolution of 3D T-S structures from continuous surface observations. The model ingests multi-day satellite SST and SSHA sequences and predicts subsequent 3D T-S states. Its end-to-end architecture couples a 2D encoder with a 3D decoder, while a long-range spatio-temporal attention module links surface signals to subsurface structure, improving mapping fidelity and forecast skill. In reanalysis-based evaluations, 3D-OceanNet more faithfully reproduces the reanalysis fields than competing models, achieving lower RMSE and MAE and reduced MAPE for 1-8-day forecasts in the South China Sea and the Northwest Pacific. Independent validation with Argo profiles shows RMSE gaps relative to GLORYS reanalysis data of 0.1-0.2 °C (temperature) and 0.04-0.07 PSU (salinity), supporting accuracy, reliability, and generalization. Together, these results demonstrate that surface observations can underpin credible forecasts of 3D oceanic T-S fields and offer practical pathways for remote-sensing applications.
