AB  - Hyperspectral image (HSI) classification under few-shot scenarios remains a challenging task due to the high dimensionality of HSI data and the scarcity of labeled samples. Traditional convolutional neural networks (CNNs) struggle to capture long-range spectral-spatial dependencies due to limited local receptive fields, while pure Transformers suffer from O((HW)2) high computational complexity and weak local feature extraction capability, making them poorly adaptive to few-shot HSI classification. To address these issues, we propose a parallel global convolution-enhanced spatial chunk Transformer network (PGC-SCTN), which realizes progressive spectral-spatial feature optimization via three core modules. Specifically, a deep convolutional embedding (DCE) module mines high-dimensional local fine-grained spectral-spatial features of HSI. A parallel global feature enhancement extraction (PGFE) module then adopts horizontal-vertical separated convolution to efficiently capture long-range spectral-spatial dependencies, compensating for the global modeling deficiency of CNNs. Furthermore, a spatial chunk Transformer (SCT) module decomposes 2-D self-attention into 1-D chunk computation along the height and width directions, reducing the computational complexity of global dependency modeling from O((HW)2) to O(HW*(H + W)) and balancing global semantic refinement with computational efficiency. Experiments on four public hyperspectral datasets show that the proposed network outperforms traditional methods and state-of-the-art (SOTA) algorithms in classification performance, demonstrating superior robustness and generalization.
