AB  - The rapid advancement of satellite technologies in recent years has significantly boosted the use of Remote Sensing (RS) techniques in geological research. As a result, large-scale geological surveys are now being conducted using multispectral and even hyperspectral data acquired through Remote Sensing (RS) and Geographic Information Systems (GIS) technologies, including data from satellites and drones. Methodologies have been developed that enable the identification of mineral types, fault lines, mineral deposits, and other geological features through RS. Remote sensing is also widely employed to analyze and predict geological hazards such as earthquakes, landslides, and volcanic activity. Furthermore, RS techniques like LIDAR represent some of the most advanced tools available to geologists for studying landforms and geological processes such as erosion and sediment deposition (Sadek and Hassan, Application of remote sensing in lithological discrimination and geological mapping of Precambrian basement rocks in the Eastern Desert of Egypt. In: Proceedings of the 33rd Asian Conference on Remote Sensing, Pattaya, Thailand, 2012). It is also worth noting that numerous projects are being carried out using satellite imagery and supplementary data to identify surface compositions and anomalies, which can aid in locating areas with potential mineral resources. In general, the detection of minerals and alteration zones relies on classification techniques, which are among the core functions of remote sensing (RS) (Long et al., J Sens Actuator Netw 11(3):50, 2022). This article explores the use of Artificial Neural Networks (ANNs) for identifying mineral alteration features and compares their performance with traditional statistical methods.
