AB  - Accurate bathymetric mapping of shallow inland waters is critical for water resource management, sediment budgeting, and hydrodynamic modeling, yet repeated in-situ surveys are costly and often impractical. This study assesses the potential of medium-resolution multispectral satellite imagery (Sentinel-2 and Landsat-8) integrated with machine learning to generate high-quality bathymetry for Chah Nimeh No. 3 reservoir in southeastern Iran. Four widely used algorithms (Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP), Random Forest (RF), Support Vector Regression (SVR), and XGBoost) were trained and evaluated using 52,000 in-situ water depth measurements collected via a remotely operated hydrographic survey vehicle. The MLP model achieved the highest accuracy, with Landsat-8 inputs yielding RMSE ≈ 0.82 m (R2 ≈ 0.97) and Sentinel-2 inputs RMSE ≈ 1.10 m (R2≈ 0.95), and was selected for sensor performance comparison. Hyperparameters, including the number of hidden layers, neurons per layer, and learning rate, were optimized via systematic grid search, identifying sensor-specific configurations (Sentinel-2: 9 layers, 16 neurons/layer, LR = 1e−3; Landsat-8: 11 layers, 16 neurons/layer, LR = 1e−4).Both sensors demonstrated strong predictive capability; however, the Landsat-8 model incorporating all optical and thermal infrared bands outperformed Sentinel-2. Landsat-8 achieved MAD = 0.50 m, RMSE = 0.78 m, and MAPE = 9.6 %, while Sentinel-2 produced MAD = 0.58 m, RMSE = 0.88 m, and MAPE = 10.4 % (test data: 30 % of total in-situ points). Direct spatial comparison of final bathymetric products showed a high coefficient of determination (R2 = 0.90) and minimal mean difference (+0.11 m, STD = 1.7 m), indicating strong agreement between the two datasets. Correlation analysis revealed unexpectedly strong associations between Landsat-8 thermal bands and depth (Band 10: R2 = 0.52; Band 11: R2 = 0.53). To quantify their contribution, two MLP models were compared: one using all Landsat-8 bands and another excluding thermal bands. Thermal inclusion improved performance substantially (R2 = 0.98, RMSE = 0.78 m) relative to the thermal-excluded model (R2 = 0.93, RMSE = 1.30 m), representing a 67 % increase in RMSE without thermal data. This enhancement likely reflects depth-linked thermal patterns driven by differential heat capacity, sediment heating in shallow zones, and depth-dependent sediment–water heat exchanges.These findings demonstrate that integrating freely available medium-resolution multispectral and thermal imagery with MLP can yield sub-metre bathymetric products (<5% error relative to an 18 m depth range in Chah Nimeh reservoir) in optically clear shallow lakes, providing a cost-effective and operationally valuable tool for monitoring and managing data-sparse inland reservoirs.
