AB  - Frequent natural and anthropogenic disasters occurring on Earth pose significant threats to human life and property. Timely and accurate responses to these disasters are crucial for mitigating the damage they cause. However, existing disaster detection methods are typically designed for a single category and rely on multi-temporal imagery, making them inadequate for real-time, multi-class disaster detection in on-orbit scenarios. In this study, we design a multi-hazard detection framework based on a single satellite image, aiming to ensure both high detection accuracy across multiple disaster categories and computational efficiency. Specifically, we investigate the advantages and disadvantages of traditional threshold methods primarily based on handcrafted spectral features and the currently popular deep learning approaches for disaster recognition tasks. Based on this, we propose a novel disaster detection framework called SSF-GNN. This method combines the strengths of both approaches by integrating predefined spectral features with semantic features extracted by deep learning models, and then using a graph neural network to reason over these features to determine whether a disaster has occurred in the remote sensing imagery. Moreover, due to the current lack of multi-hazard detection datasets based on multispectral imagery, we have constructed a new large-scale dataset containing six types of disasters, named S2MHD. Our method achieves an overall accuracy of 88.37% on this dataset, significantly outperforming existing state-of-the-art methods.
