AB  - Turbidity, a fundamental water quality parameter, profoundly influences aquatic ecosystems by regulating light penetration, primary productivity, and the fate of nutrients and pollutants. Despite prior large-scale optical water-quality studies of U.S. rivers, systematic, multi-decadal analyses integrating Landsat surface reflectance, machine-learning retrieval, and ecoregion-scale driver attribution have remained limited. This study developed and validated a long-term Landsat-based turbidity reconstruction framework for major rivers of the conterminous United States from 1984 to 2025. By integrating harmonized Landsat 5/7/8/9 surface reflectance (Collection 2 Tier 1) with river geometry from the Surface Water and Ocean Topography Mission River Database (SWORD), named after the SWOT satellite mission, and in-situ turbidity measurements from the USGS, we established a robust Random Forest (RF) regression model (validation R2 = 0.95, RMSE = 21.25 NTU) for turbidity estimation, outperforming three other machine-learning algorithms. The RF model was further evaluated using strict spatiotemporal transferability tests, including chronological hold-out, shared-site, new-site, and leave-one-region-out (LORO) spatial cross-validation. Model interpretability was rigorously validated using SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) analysis, confirming that the model learned physically meaningful relationships. Our study revealed distinct spatial patterns in river turbidity, with generally higher values in the Southern Plains (SPL) and Temperate Plains (TPL) ecoregions and lower values in the Western Mountains (WMT) and Xeric (XER) regions. Moreover, we detected a heterogeneous but overall declining trend in turbidity over time, particularly in historically high-turbidity areas. Model diagnostics indicate that predictive performance is higher in mid- to high-turbidity waters, and the reconstructed dataset should be interpreted primarily at regional and long-term scales. To explore the drivers of these changes, we integrated climatic, land cover, and dam data into the analysis. The results show that anthropogenic factors, including dams (∼33% contribution), forest cover (∼27%, reflecting the absence of disturbance), and urban/built-up areas (∼17%), are the primary contributors to turbidity variance at the national scale, far outweighing the influence of climatic variables (each generally < 5%). Further investigation of ten representative dammed rivers indicated that dam construction is associated with significant downstream reductions in turbidity, with the effect typically stronger for taller dams but modulated by local topography. As an exploratory application, framed as an illustrative case study rather than a primary finding, we further developed a turbidity-linked hydro-morphodynamic sensitivity indicator that combines turbidity decline and velocity-related channel proxies. This indicator is intended to provide a preliminary reference for identifying reaches where sediment reduction may coincide with enhanced hydraulic forcing; independent geomorphic validation is required before any hazard-related interpretations can be drawn.
