AB  - Assessing changes in land use/land cover in urban areas, primarily driven by human needs, is critical in planning and managing future land use. Within this context, machine learning techniques can be effectively employed. In this study, machine learning techniques were used to classify the land use/land cover of the central settlements and surrounding areas of Yalova Province, which lost a significant portion of its built-up areas due to the 7.4-magnitude earthquake in 1999. Classification was carried out using the Classification and Regression Tree, Random Forest, and Support Vector Machine algorithms on the Google Earth Engine platform and Convolutional Neural Network model on Google Colaboratory based on Sentinel-2 satellite imagery from 2018 and 2024, to determine the most suitable algorithm. The integration of machine learning algorithms with an ensemble classification approach was tested to enhance classification accuracy, resulting in highly reliable land use maps (kappa: 0.984 for 2018; 0.990 for 2024). Over the six years, a 10.74% increase was observed, primarily in built-up areas. The future land use/land cover for 2036 was simulated using the Artificial Neural Network method. The accuracy of the simulation was evaluated using the Receiver Operating Characteristic curve, yielding a high reliability with an Area Under the Curve value of 0.813. The analysis revealed that the land-use change trend observed between 2018 and 2024 is projected to persist, with grasslands, water bodies, and areas of dense vegetation transitioning into built-up areas. This finding suggests a progressive decline in natural vegetation over time, with the potential for irreversible loss unless effective, ecologically grounded management strategies are implemented.
