AB  - An integrated framework that combines Small Baseline Subset Interferometric Synthetic Aperture Radar (SBAS - InSAR) with an optimized Convolutional Neural Network-Long Short-Term Memory (CNN-LSTM) model‚ enhanced by an Improved Particle Swarm Optimization (IPSO) algorithm‚ is proposed to improve the monitoring and prediction of tailings pond deformation dynamics. This approach was applied to monitor ground deformation at a lead-zinc mine tailings pond in Yunnan Province using SBAS - InSAR technology‚ leveraging 97 Sentinel 1A satellite images collected from January 2021 to September 2024. Vertical deformation results were rigorously validated against Global Navigation Satellite System (GNSS) measurements. The validation confirmed an annual deformation rate of - 58. 56 mm / a‚ a cumulative settlement of - 233. 76 mm‚ and a maximum absolute error of 1. 90 mm / a‚ highlighting the accuracy of the monitoring method. For deformation prediction‚ cumulative settlement time series data from seven representative feature points were employed in the modeling process. Notably‚ the Improved Particle Swarm Optimization (IPSO) algorithm was utilized to optimize three crucial hyperparameters of the CNN - LSTM model: the number of hidden layers‚ the initial learning rate‚ and the L2 regularization coefficient. Thanks to its dynamic adaptive inertia weights and learning factors‚ the IPSO algorithm effectively minimized the root mean square error (RMSE) ‚ converging to the optimal hyperparameter set after just 21 iterations. To thoroughly evaluate the performance of the IPSO - CNN - LSTM model‚ comparisons were conducted against several benchmark models‚ including standalone CNN models‚ standalone LSTM models‚ and unoptimized CNN - LSTM models. Performance evaluation was carried out using key metrics‚ including Mean Absolute Error (MAE) ‚ Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) ‚ Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) ‚ and Coefficient of Determination (R2 ) . The IPSO - CNN - LSTM model demonstrated a significant advantage‚ achieving an R2 value exceeding 97% at all test points. Additionally‚ the RMSE was reduced by at least 51. 6% compared to the unoptimized CNN - LSTM model. Importantly‚ the optimized model maintained high prediction accuracy even in the presence of rainfall fluctuations. These significant improvements underscore the critical role of IPSO optimization in substantially enhancing the inherent spatiotemporal feature extraction capabilities of the CNN - LSTM architecture. The developed SBAS - InSAR - IPSO - CNN - LSTM framework offers a novel‚ robust‚ and accurate solution for tailings pond risk management.
