AB  - With the exponential improvement of earth observation capabilities, the primary challenge in remote sensing has shifted from massive data acquisition to efficient information extraction. Currently, user attention has increasingly turned to monitoring the dynamic changes of objects of interest. Traditional change detection paradigms usually require strict pixel-level registration and radiometric consistency correction for multi-temporal images. However, their high computational cost and sensitivity to registration accuracy make them difficult to meet the demands of time-critical applications. Inspired by the human visual system, which can perceive changes between images without requiring precise pixel alignment or radiometric normalization, namely spot-the-differences, this paper proposes a novel object-level change detection framework that reduces the dependence on strict pixel-level spatial registration. Instead of directly detecting “changes,” which are often difficult to define precisely, the proposed framework identifies and matches unchanged objects across image pairs, thereby indirectly inferring the change set. The core of this framework is a background-aware matching network based on deformable convolution. It incorporates a contrastive learning strategy and introduces a classification-oriented contrastive loss function. This enables the network to model elastic geometric correspondences and robustly associate the same semantic regions directly from misaligned image pairs without performing explicit pixel-level registration during inference. To verify the generality of the proposed method, we constructed a large-scale object-level change detection dataset. The method was integrated with multiple object detection frameworks, including Single-Stage, Two-Stage, Anchor-free, and DETR-based models, to evaluate its robustness and adaptability across different architectures. Experimental results show that, in challenging misaligned scenarios, the proposed method achieves a mean average precision (mAP) of 48.7% on the ATSS framework. This significantly outperforms image-difference-based methods. Moreover, it reduces the false alarm rate from approximately 90.0% in the CYWS method to 12.8%. These results provide a new perspective and exploration for relation learning on misaligned temporal data. The code and dataset for this work will be available on GitHub.
