N1  - EID: 2-s2.0-105043127041
N1  - Export Date: 05 October 2026
N1  - Cited By: 0
AB  - Objective Hyperspectral images (HSIs) are characterized by capturing hundreds of continuous narrow bands covering the visible to near-infrared spectrum. They can form nearly continuous spectral curves, enabling precise identification of ground materials. Consequently, HSIs have shown significant application value in fields such as precision agriculture, disaster monitoring, mineral exploration, and military reconnaissance. However, constrained by the trade-off between the luminous flux of imaging systems and the physical limits of sensor hardware, hyperspectral imaging often struggles to maintain high spatial resolution while ensuring high spectral resolution. In contrast, multispectral images (MSIs) possess higher spatial resolution and imaging efficiency but contain only a few discrete spectral bands. Therefore, fusing a low spatial resolution HSI (LR-HSI) with a high spatial resolution MSI (HR-MSI) of the same scene to generate a high spatial resolution HSI (HR-HSI) has become a critical research topic in remote sensing. Existing fusion methods still face challenges. Traditional model-driven methods, such as matrix factorization and tensor decomposition, rely on handcrafted priors and struggle to model complex non-linear mappings. Deep learning-based methods, particularly convolutional neural networks (CNNs), are limited by local receptive fields, failing to capture long-range spatial dependencies effectively. While Transformers offer global modeling capabilities via self-attention, their quadratic computational complexity hinders efficiency when processing high-dimensional spectral data. Recently, state space models (SSMs) like Mamba have shown promise with linear complexity, but their autoregressive nature often limits the capture of complete global context in a single forward pass. Furthermore, most existing methods employ simple concatenation for feature fusion, neglecting the deep cross-modal interaction required to balance spatial detail recovery and spectral consistency. To address these limitations, this paper proposes a multi-scale spatial-spectral interaction fusion network based on Mamba-Transformer (MTFNet). The primary objective is to construct a scale-adaptive framework that leverages the efficiency of Mamba for high-resolution spatial details and the global semantic capability of Transformers for low-resolution features, thereby achieving superior fusion performance. Methods The proposed MTFNet adopts a dual-branch U-Net structure for encoding and decoding as its backbone. The network processes the spatial and spectral inputs separately to ensure the integrity of the original information. The core innovation lies in the design of specific cross-modal interaction strategies tailored to different feature scales. First, at the high-resolution feature stage (Stage 1 and Stage 5), where the spatial dimensions are large, we introduce the modality-guided Mamba (MGM) module. The MGM module incorporates a modality-guided 2D selective scan (MGSS2D) mechanism. Unlike traditional SSMs that model sequences independently, MGSS2D utilizes one modality (the guidance modality) to dynamically generate system parameters that modulate the state space evolution of the other modality (the target modality). This design extends SSM from single-modal modeling to a dual modal collaborative inference architecture, enabling efficient recovery of spatial details under the constraint of global spectral context with linear complexity. Second, at the low-resolution feature stage (Stages 2 ‒ 4), where semantic information is concentrated, we design the cross-modal interaction Transformer (CMIT). The core of CMIT is the cross-modal interaction attention (CMIA) mechanism. Instead of self-attention within a single modality, CMIA facilitates bidirectional global interaction by using the Query and Key vectors from one modality to retrieve Value vectors from the other. This allows the spatial features to aggregate relevant global spectral information and vice versa, ensuring deep semantic alignment and information complementarity. Finally, the outputs from the decoder are fused via element-wise addition, followed by a lightweight spectral attention module. This module recalibrates the channel weights based on the global statistics of the initial fusion result to further enhance the spectral fidelity of key bands. The network is trained end-to-end using a mean squared error (MSE) loss function. Results and Discussions The proposed MTFNet is evaluated on five standard datasets: Pavia University (PU), Pavia Center (PC), Urban, Botswana, and Washington DC Mall (WDCM). We compare MTFNet with nine mainstream comparison methods, including traditional optimization-based methods (CNMF, LTTR) and deep learning-based methods (TFNet, ResTFNet, SSFCNN, ConSSFCNN, SSRNet, MCTNet, FusionMamba). Quantitative experiments demonstrate that MTFNet achieves state-of-the-art performance across all datasets. For instance, on the WDCM dataset, MTFNet achieves the best results in terms of root mean square error (RMSE), peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR), and relative global error (ERGAS). Specifically, the PSNR reaches 47.3492 dB, which is significantly higher than that of the Transformer-based MCTNet (46.4498 dB) and the Mamba-based FusionMamba (46.0458 dB). Similarly, on the Botswana dataset, which features low spatial resolution, MTFNet achieves the lowest RMSE of 0.3336 and the highest PSNR of 40.7018 dB, indicating its robustness in recovering spatial structures even when original spatial information is scarce. In terms of spectral consistency, MTFNet consistently achieves the lowest spectral angle mapper (SAM) values across most datasets, proving its superior ability to preserve spectral fidelity. Qualitative analysis further validates these findings. The error maps for the PC and WDCM datasets show that while competitors like SSRNet and MCTNet exhibit scattered high-response residuals in background regions, MTFNet presents a more uniform and low-amplitude error distribution. This indicates that the fused images generated by MTFNet are numerically closer to the ground truth. Spectral fidelity is also analyzed by comparing the spectral curves of randomly selected pixels. The curves generated by MTFNet align closely with the reference ground truth in terms of peak positions and reflectance intensity, whereas other deep learning methods often exhibit spectral shifts or attenuation. Ablation studies on the WDCM dataset confirm the effectiveness of each component. The baseline U-Net (A0) performs the worst. Introducing the MGM module (B1) and the CMIT module (B2) significantly improves performance compared to using standard Mamba or Transformer blocks, verifying the importance of the proposed modality-guided and cross-modal interaction mechanisms. Swapping the scales of MGM and CMIT (C1) results in performance degradation, validating our scale-adaptive design strategy: using Mamba for high-resolution features and Transformer for low-resolution semantic features is the optimal configuration. Complexity analysis reveals that MTFNet offers an excellent trade-off between performance and computational cost. While it has a higher parameter count (18.7389×106) than the lightweight SSRNet, it outperforms it by a large margin (~8 dB in PSNR on WDCM). Compared to other large-scale models like FusionMamba, MTFNet achieves higher fusion quality with comparable computational complexity. Conclusions In this study, we propose MTFNet, a novel multi-scale spatial-spectral interaction fusion network that effectively integrates the strengths of Mamba and Transformer. By deploying the MGM at high-resolution stages and the CMIT at low-resolution stages, the network successfully addresses the shortcomings of existing methods in modeling cross-modal dependencies and preserving spectral information. The MGM module efficiently handles long sequences of spatial details via a dynamic parameter generation mechanism, while the CMIT module ensures rigorous global semantic alignment using bidirectional attention. Comprehensive experiments on five public datasets demonstrate that MTFNet significantly improves the spatial detail recovery and spectral consistency of the fused images compared to nine mainstream comparison methods. The results confirm that the proposed hybrid architecture provides a robust and effective solution for high-quality hyperspectral and multispectral image fusion. Future work will focus on optimizing the model structure for lightweight deployment to enhance its applicability in real-time remote sensing tasks.
