AB  - Improved data on conservation practices in agriculture would help to boost understanding of their impacts on crop productivity, environmental outcomes, and climate resilience. Despite ongoing adoption of several practices in many regions, public information is typically limited to very coarse estimates of practice prevalence at a frequency of once or twice per decade. Remote sensing has shown potential value for mapping many practices, but existing methods have relied on calibration with field-scale practice data which are unavailable in most locations and years. In this study, we present a Scale Transfer with Pseudo-Labelling (STaPL) framework for mapping agricultural practices without field-scale data. Using conservation tillage as a case study, we combine Landsat earth observations, elevation data, and county-level tillage census data. We first generate pseudo-labels for tillage types in each county and year using publicly available county-level tillage statistics. These labels are then iteratively updated using predictions from a deep multilayer perceptron (MLP) model. The final pseudo-labels are used to train a random forest classifier for tillage mapping. Comparison of STaPL predictions with a ground-based tillage dataset that spans eight years and more than 25,000 field-level observations demonstrates that STaPL has comparable overall performance (Accuracy = 75%) and stronger temporal generalizability than reference models trained using field-level tillage data, although some temporal variability is still observed in STaPL-based models. Class-wise evaluation shows that STaPL performs better on capturing low-intensity tillage class (F1 = 80.29.%), while underperforming the field-supervised baseline by about 3% on the high-intensity class (F1 = 65.82%). Using the STaPL framework, we generate a dataset of annual 30-m tillage maps for the twelve U.S. Midwestern states in 2001–2023. These maps exhibit strong agreement with official statistics on tillage practices at both the county (r2 = 0.65) and state level (r2 = 0.83). The STaPL tillage maps provide a useful proxy for studying conservation tillage adoption in the U.S. and the STaPL framework can be readily extended to other agricultural practices and regions to support decision making in agroecosystems. The 30-m STaPL tillage maps are available publicly at https://github.com/yuchima8/STaPL.
