N1  - Export Date: 05 October 2026
N1  - Cited By: 0
AB  - Objective In the field of fine classification of hyperspectral images (HSI), how to fully mine and utilize the rich information contained in the data has always been a primary challenge. Although existing deep learning models have made significant progress, they often treat spectral and spatial features as independent modalities processed in isolation, neglecting the deep correlations and cross-modal dependencies between them. This “feature decoupling” approach often leads to the loss of discriminative information when dealing with complex scenarios-such as “same object with different spectra” or “different objects with the same spectrum”-resulting in suboptimal classification accuracy. Therefore, this study aims to overcome the limitations of independent spectral-spatial modeling and construct a joint characterization mechanism capable of deep spectral-spatial collaboration, ultimately achieving a more precise interpretation of complex ground objects. Methods This paper proposes a hyperspectral image classification network termed dual-branch interactive attention fusion (DBIAF). The network employs a dual-branch architecture to synergistically capture multi-dimensional features. Specifically, the spectral branch adopts a multi-scale convolution module comprising convolution kernels of varying sizes, designed to extract spectral details and capture inter-band long-range dependencies at multiple granularities. In the spatial branch, a stacked dense atrous spatial pyramid pooling (DenseASPP) module is introduced. By cascading atrous convolutions with different dilation rates, this module constructs hierarchical receptive fields to capture multi-scale spatial context information while preserving feature resolution. Furthermore, this paper innovatively designs an improved spectral-spatial interactive attention mechanism, which recalibrates and enhances features through cross-modal interaction. Finally, a residual gated fusion mechanism is utilized to dynamically adjust weights based on feature importance, thereby achieving adaptive deep fusion of spectral and spatial information. Results and Discussions Extensive experiments on three mainstream public hyperspectral datasets (Indian Pines, Pavia University, and Salinas Valley) verify the effectiveness of the proposed method. Experimental results demonstrate that the overall accuracy (OA) of the DBIAF model reaches 97.82%, 98.81%, and 97.53%, respectively. Furthermore, key performance indicators, including the Kappa coefficient and average accuracy (AA), significantly outperform those of current state-of-the-art deep learning models. Particularly in boundary regions and scenes with complex spatial distributions, the model exhibits enhanced robustness. The generated classification maps show distinct advantages in preserving the structural integrity of land covers, validating the efficacy of the interactive attention and dynamic fusion strategies in feature enhancement. Conclusions The DBIAF network proposed in this paper successfully overcomes the limitations of traditional methods in the joint representation of spatial and spectral features by leveraging a dual-branch collaborative architecture and a cross-modal interaction mechanism. This method not only effectively preserves the fidelity of spectral information and the integrity of spatial information but also significantly enhances the model’s feature representation capability for complex objects through deep fusion. Consequently, these findings provide a novel perspective on solving the problem of insufficient spectral-spatial feature fusion in hyperspectral image classification, while offering efficient and reliable technical solutions for practical applications in precision agriculture, urban planning, and environmental monitoring.
