AB  - Social unrest, a collective expression of discontent, is a salient indicator of deeper societal problems. Its prediction, especially at higher spatial resolutions, is challenging due to its complex dependence on socioeconomic, political, and environmental factors. Accurately forecasting social unrest will provide a better understanding of its drivers and help develop mitigation strategies. This study evaluates the role of geographic context in predicting social unrest by proposing two different and novel approaches to predict social unrest using machine learning techniques: (a) a geographic context-independent model using eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), which predicts unrest based on features at a single point, and (b) a geographic context-dependent model using a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN), which incorporates features from the surrounding geospatial neighborhood. The models use remote sensing imagery and rasters of geographic, socio-demographic, and economic variables (and their proxies). In addition, the study presents a detailed and unique analysis to identify the significance of the features in predicting social unrest using Shapley values. Another key innovation of this research is the estimation of the confidence in the models’ predictions using a Monte Carlo dropout technique. This provides valuable insights into the model’s reliability and uncertainty in prediction as a function of geographic space. Results show that VIIRS nighttime light and land use are among the significant predictors of social unrest. Furthermore, prediction performance comparison shows that context-dependent CNNs outperform the context-independent XGBoost model in accuracy and F1 scores in both binary and multiclass classification tasks. The findings demonstrate the potential of machine learning in accurately understanding complex social science processes.
