AB  - Reliable precipitation estimation over complex terrain requires the integration of complementary information from rain gauges, weather radar, and satellite products. Effective data fusion remains challenging due to scale inconsistencies, spatial heterogeneity, and source-dependent uncertainties. We propose a transparent two-stage deep-learning–Bayesian multi-source precipitation fusion framework (DBMPF) integrating spatiotemporal deep-learning (DL) correction with probabilistic ensemble calibration. The first stage employs a hybrid 3-dimensional convolutional neural network (3D-CNN) and convolutional long short-term memory (ConvLSTM) architecture to extract station-centered spatiotemporal features from multi-source data cubes and to produce corrected member ensemble members corresponding to different source combinations. The second stage applies Bayesian model averaging (BMA) to merge these members into a calibrated predictive distribution through the expectation–maximization (EM) algorithm. A Box-Cox transformation is introduced to better satisfy Gaussian residual assumptions, facilitating uncertainty quantification through prediction intervals and probabilistic evaluation metrics. The DBMPF framework is assessed in the Tunxi River basin (Anhui, China) during May–September, trained on 2019–2020, and validated in 2022 for validation at daily, hourly, and half-hourly temporal resolutions. Across all scales, DBMPF surpasses both the source products and benchmark approaches. At the daily scale, the correlation coefficient (CC) increases from 0.942 for radar to 0.994,root mean square error (RMSE) decreases from 5.231 to 1.685 mm/day, and Kling-Gupta efficiency (KGE) rises from 0.750 to 0.972, accompanied by negligible bias. At sub-daily resolutions, improvements are observed in event detection performance as measured by critical success index (CSI) and false alarm ratio (FAR), and the framework achieves superior probabilistic performance for 95% prediction intervals, attaining the lowest continuous ranked probability score (CRPS), PICP values closest to the nominal 0.95 level, and the smallest absolute coverage error (ACE) across precipitation intensity categories while maintaining relatively narrow mean prediction interval width (MPIW). A progressive gauge-density experiment involving 18–45 stations reveals substantial performance gains under sparse observational networks and diminishing returns at moderate to high densities, offering guidance for cost-efficient network configuration. The proposed DBMPF framework provides a modular approach for generating high-resolution deterministic and probabilistic precipitation estimates, with particular potential for flood-focused hydrological applications requiring uncertainty-aware precipitation inputs.
