AB  - Waterbody mapping has always been a research hotspot, with utmost significance in water resource management. Although deep learning approaches have achieved remarkable accomplishments, most of them are designed for high-resolution images, with limited adaptability to the city scale, and suffered from serious misjudgments, frequent breakages, and rough morphologies in complex scenarios. To overcome these obstacles, this paper proposes an innovative connectivity preservation and boundary refinement network (CB-Net), to precisely delineate waterbodies at the city scale using medium-resolution remote sensing images, which explicitly focuses on geometrical characteristic mining of waterbodies. Specifically, with regard to frequent breakages and rough morphologies, an effective dense dynamic snake convolution module is developed, to deeply learn felicitous geometrical features for various waterbodies and aggregate hierarchical expressions to preserve connectivity, which enables continuous and detailed waterbody extraction. In addition, to improve boundary localization and morphology expressivity, a valid high-frequency multiscale feature fusion module is designed as skip connections, to learn multidirectional high-frequency information and leverage multiscale directional expressions to generate structure-and-position-aware features, thus facilitating fine-grained segmentations. A challenging waterbody dataset based on Sentinel-2 images (S2-Water) is constructed for performance validation, which compensates for the scarcity of specialized datasets and advances future researches. Extensive experimental results illustrate that CB-Net significantly outperformed the other state-of-the-art alternatives to yield promising results for Wuhan City and Jinan City, indicating its good adaptability and generalizability to various waterbodies in complex scenarios. Generally, CB-Net achieved a mean F1-sore of 88.69% and IoU (Intersection over union) of 79.79%, which well satisfies applicational requirements.
