AB  - Semantic segmentation of high-resolution remote sensing imagery is pivotal for topographic mapping and environmental monitoring but remains challenged by significant scale variations, shadow occlusions, and high inter-class similarity. While hybrid Transformer-CNN architectures address multi-scale modeling, they often suffer from quadratic computational complexity and coarse granularity. To alleviate these issues, this paper proposes a Lightweight Multi-scale perception and Global semantic calibration Network (LMGNet), featuring a Multi-scale Context Decoder (MCDecoder) and a Global semantic calibration strategy. Specifically, we introduce a Multi-scale Mamba (MSMamba) module. Departing from the single-scale scanning of standard Mamba, MSMamba implements a “decoupled-then-coupled” strategy to efficiently capture both panorama-centric long-range context and region-centric mid-range dependencies, facilitating robust cross-spatial semantic interaction. Furthermore, to rectify class distribution bias, we propose a Density estimation-Guided Segmentation Head (DGSHead). It utilizes auxiliary density priors to perform global semantic calibration on decoder features via a cross-attention mechanism. Experimental results demonstrate that LMGNet outperforms most state-of-the-art methods in terms of both segmentation performance and efficiency on the ISPRS Vaihingen, Potsdam and LoveDA datasets.
