AB  - Rapid glacier retreat in the Himalayas is accelerating glacial lake formation and expansion, increasing potential GLOF risk. Frequent, large-area mapping is needed to track rapidly changing lakes, but the computational cost of deep learning models limits high-frequency updates in resource-constrained mountain regions. To address this limitation, we propose GCE-Net (Glacial-lake Compact Efficient Neural Network), a compact segmentation framework designed to improve efficiency while maintaining accuracy (F1 score = 94.43%). GCE-Net uses a ResNet-34 encoder and a UNet++ decoder improved with Ghost modules and Efficient Channel Attention (ECA). After training, we apply a depth-guided structured pruning strategy to the full architecture without additional fine-tuning. Results show that the pruning level L3 sub-network (L3) provides a favorable balance between accuracy and efficiency, reducing parameters by 62.7% and computation by 30.6%, while incurring only a minor change in accuracy (F1 decrease < 0.3%). The trained GCE-Net was applied to the Southeastern Tibetan Plateau (SETP) to generate a 2023 glacial lake inventory, including 6,455 lakes with a total area of 400.18 km2. Comparison with published 1990 and 2018 inventories indicates faster lake growth after 2018, with recent increases dominated by small-to-medium lakes. For lakes that persisted from 1990 to 2023, net area change was positive within 0–2 km of glaciers but negative beyond 2 km, and the average per-lake change generally weakened with distance.
