AB  - Classifying fruits according to their ripeness for storage, transport, and marketing is an effective method to extend shelf life and enhance fruit quality. To achieve this classification, it is essential to select the appropriate ripeness features for the target fruits and construct a suitable classification model based on these features. However, judgement based on single-modal information often leads to inaccurate conclusions because of incomplete feature expression. Thus, a feature-decision fusion network for fruit ripeness classification (FDFR-Net) utilizing multimodal features is proposed in this paper. It processes information from three modalities through a two-stage fusion method at the feature level and decision level to extract, fuse, and analyze fruit information. Mango was selected as the experimental subject and non-destructive multimodal data collection including Red-Green-Blue image, hyperspectral image and impedance features was performed. Ripeness labels were annotated by establishing an objective maturity grading standard. The objectivity of the dataset was verified by analyzing the hardness and soluble solids content. After training and testing, the proposed FDFR-Net demonstrated outstanding performance in fruit ripeness classification, achieving an accuracy of 94.23 %, precision of 94.45 %, recall of 94.25 %, and an F1 score of 0.9435. These results demonstrate an improvement over other modal methods, suggesting the method’s potential competitive advantage in the industry. Furthermore, this research developed a practical application based on FDFR-Net, offering a feasible methodology and recommendations for fruit ripeness grading strategies, which may contribute to reducing waste and loss in the post-harvest process.
