AB  - Hyperspectral image classification (HSIC) is widely used in many remote sensing applications such as agriculture monitoring, urban planning, mineral identification and mineral identification. However, the HSIC is challenging due to intricate structure of images, lower spatial-spectral correlation, higher spectral bands, redundancy in the spectral bands and huge variability in band information. This paper presents the HSIC using deep convolution neural network (DCNN) to improve the spatial-spectral correlation in the spectral bands of hyperspectral images. The outcomes of the DCNN are analyzed for the PSO based spectral band selection technique. The results of the HSIC are evaluated on the Indian Pines dataset and the PSO-DCNN achieved overall recall of 91.92%, precision of 94.86%, F1-score of 91.79% and accuracy of 92.12% for Indian Pines.
