AB  - Abstract: Accurate quantitative prediction of ion concentrations in salt lakes is crucial for mineral resource assessment and water quality monitoring. However, for complex ionic systems, the optimal selection of remote sensing sensors and predictive models remains an open challenge. Taking the ion-rich Lake South Hobson as a case study, this research synergistically combined high-resolution hyperspectral imagery from the Chinese GF-5B and ZY1-02E satellites with ground-based measurements to systematically develop and compare random forest (RF), extreme gradient boosting (XGBoost), multilayer perceptron (MLP), and artificial neural network (ANN) models for predicting the concentrations of seven key ions: K+, Ca2+, Na+, Mg2+, Li+, B3+, and Cl−. The results demonstrate that all models achieved strong predictive performance, with the coefficient of determination (R2) reaching up to 0.909; nevertheless, significant performance disparities were evident across ions, sensors, and models. Further in-depth analysis reveals that the challenge in predicting the concentrations of ions such as Mg2+ is closely linked to their high background levels and distinctive spectral signatures. Overall, the GF-5B data exhibited a clear overall advantage, while the ZY1-02E data showed notable robustness throughout the modelling process. The XGBoost models generally delivered consistently strong and stable performance. This study not only fills a critical gap in the remote-sensing-based monitoring of salt lake ion concentrations in this region, but also validates the feasibility of leveraging a constellation of domestically developed satellites for salt lake monitoring, thereby offering a robust technical solution for the sustainable management of these strategic resources. Highlights: The GF-5B and ZY1-02E, two domestically developed satellites, worked in concert to perform high-resolution spectral modeling of seven ions, thereby validating the feasibility of collaborative monitoring. The study revealed that Mg2 predictions are constrained by high concentrations and spectral characteristics, explaining the differences in ion-specific performance. The study confirmed that the GF-5B offers superior accuracy, the ZY1-02E is more robust, and XGBoost delivers the most stable performance, thereby providing an optimal selection strategy.
