AB  - As deep learning technology continues to evolve, deep neural networks have achieved significant breakthroughs in the domain of semantic segmentation of remote sensing images (RSI). However, the deepening of network architectures inevitably leads to a substantial increase in the consumption of computational resources, which is highly detrimental in scenarios where resources are limited. To tackle this issue, this paper presents a novel knowledge distillation method based on features and pixels (FPKD), which aims to improve the real-time performance of model prediction while maintaining a high level of segmentation effect. Initially, a triplet probability map distillation (TPMD) module is proposed. This model can effectively guide the student network to focus on the key processing areas of the teacher network, and significantly improve the learning efficiency of the student network by simulating the probability distribution map of the teacher network. Subsequently, a similarity distillation (SD) module is presented, enabling the student network to assimilate the interactions and connections between pixels. Finally, in order to better adjust the bias and variance in the learning process, an adaptive loss function is designed to optimize the training process by dynamically adjusting the loss weight, thereby enhancing the model’s generalization capabilities. Extensive experiments were conducted on the Potsdam, Vaihingen, and LoveDA datasets. Experimental results show that compared with the current leading baseline model, the proposed method demonstrates better performance on multiple key performance indicators, fully verifying the efficacy and practicability of the proposed approach.
