AB  - As artificial intelligence (AI) systems are increasingly adopted for remote monitoring applications, ensuring transparency and reliability in model decision-making has become critical, particularly in safety-sensitive domains such as nuclear facility surveillance. Conventional deep learning approaches can achieve high predictive accuracy but often operate as black boxes, limiting their suitability for high-consequence decision support. To address this limitation, this study presents a remote monitoring model that integrates explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) with satellite imagery and deep learning to enhance the interpretability and trustworthiness of AI-assisted monitoring of nuclear power plants. A surrogate dataset was constructed from satellite imagery in Google Earth Pro. The model was evaluated across multiple monitoring tasks. To quantify predictive uncertainty and enhance reliability, a deep ensemble strategy was employed, and five complementary XAI methods: Grad-CAM++, the Sobol attribution method, Occlusion sensitivity, LIME, and SHAP were applied to interpret model behavior. The trained models achieved high accuracy and F1 scores across all tasks, except for anomaly detection, which proved more challenging due to the subtlety of visual indicators. Low expected calibration error values further demonstrated well-calibrated probabilistic predictions. The XAI methods were systematically assessed using MuFidelity and average stability metrics, and uncertainty-aware attribution maps were generated to identify regions of elevated prediction uncertainty. Overall, the evaluations demonstrate how explainable and uncertainty-aware deep learning can support transparent, reliable, and interpretable satellite-based monitoring of nuclear facilities.
