AB  - Hyperspectral anomaly detection (HAD) aims to identify pixels whose spectral signatures deviate from the surrounding background without requiring prior knowledge. Although autoencoder-based methods achieve promising performance, they often suffer from insufficient separation between background and anomalous samples and inadequate modeling of heterogeneous background patterns. Moreover, the strong representation capability of neural networks may cause certain anomalies to be reconstructed, resulting in missed detections. To address these limitations, we propose an attribution-driven background modeling network with context-aware anomaly suppression, termed ADANet. The proposed framework adaptively mines sample attributions, explicitly models diverse background patterns, and suppresses anomaly reconstruction. Specifically, we develop a feature-guided sample attribution mining (FGSAM) strategy that leverages representative background prototypes to infer sample attributions, enabling effective separation of multiple background categories and reliable identification of anomalies. Building on this, a spectral-feature consistent discriminative (SFCD) loss is designed to enforce intra-class compactness and inter-class separability among background features, while maintaining structural consistency between the spectral and feature domains. In addition, we propose a context-aware anomaly suppression (CAAS) module that leverages background features from the spatial neighborhoods of anomalous pixels to constrain anomaly reconstruction, driving reconstructed anomalies toward the background distribution and thereby reducing missed detections. Quantitative comparisons with state-of-the-art methods show that our approach achieves consistently high (Formula presented) values across six hyperspectral image datasets, with the highest value reaching 99.95%, and outperforms the second-best method by up to 0.70%. Qualitative comparisons further support the superiority of the proposed method.
