AB  - Cyanobacterial harmful algal blooms (CyanoHABs) critically threaten global freshwater security in lakes. Timely prediction of their magnitude and spatially explicit distribution is essential for mitigating ecological risk. Conventional mechanistic models demand massive data inputs, well-defined boundary conditions, and intensive computation, rendering them impractical for most lakes. Our proposed machine learning framework overcomes these constraints by intelligently integrating sparse monitoring stations and satellite data, and by constructing time cumulative effect variables, which are defined as the average or cumulative values of environmental variables over preceding days. Using 23 years of observations from Lake Chaohu, China, we evaluated four algorithms—Random Forest (RF), XGBoost, Artificial Neural Network (ANN), and Ridge regression, for forecasting CyanoHABs at 1/3/7-day lead times. RF achieved optimal 1-day predictions for CyanoHABs percentage (CBP) (root mean square error (RMSE) = 4.18 %, coefficient of determination (R2) = 0.81) through strategic feature selection from cumulative variables. RF outperformed other models in temporal pattern recognition and sustained robust performance across all forecast horizons (RMSE: 4.18 %–6.26 %, R2: 0.59–0.81). For spatially explicit predictions, the RF model achieved superior 1-day accuracy (RMSE = 5.05 %, F1 score = 0.84) through dynamic interpolation of sparse station data, outperforming 3/7-day forecasts. By leveraging sparse monitoring data, this computationally efficient approach enables reliable 1–7-day forecasting of spatially explicit distributions. It thus offers a transferable solution for proactive CyanoHABs management in data-sparse eutrophic lakes worldwide.
