AB  - The fusion of hyperspectral images (HSIs) and light detection and ranging (LiDAR) data has become a cornerstone in remote sensing classification, leveraging complementary spectral and elevation information for accurate land cover classification. However, when modeling complex spatial relationships under limited labeled samples, the existing graph-based approaches often suffer from a “broken topology,” i.e., a sparse and fragmented network structure that severely limits information flow. To address this limitation, in this work, we propose a novel diffusion-enhanced multimodal graph neural network (DMGNN), which takes the inherent topological advantages of graph representations to effectively model complex multimodal structures. A diffusion-enhanced multimodal graph construction module is first formulated to generate synthetic nodes that densify the fragmented topology, ensuring effective message passing even in sparse data regions. Second, a spectral clustering-guided pretraining strategy is introduced to mine the global cluster structure of the multimodal graph for superior parameter initialization. Third, a dynamic multimodal contrastive learning mechanism is constructed to enforce structural robustness and cross-modal alignment within the node embeddings. Fourth, an adaptive feature extraction and multiobjective optimization module is introduced to refine multiscale features and handle class imbalance. By flexibly utilizing topological structures, our method successfully addresses the scarcity of effective graph-based approaches. Extensive experiments on three widely used HSI and LiDAR datasets demonstrate that our proposed DMGNN outperforms the state-of-the-art methods. Notably, our method achieves an overall accuracy (OA) of 99.88% on Houston2013, 99.50% on Trento, and 95.14% on highly imbalanced Houston2018 sliced dataset.
