AB  - Land-use and land-cover change (LULC) is a critical driver of terrestrial carbon dynamics and climate change, particularly in regions undergoing rapid urbanization. This study integrates satellite imagery, land-use maps, and the Integrated Valuation of Ecosystem Services and Tradeoffs (InVEST) model to examine the spatiotemporal interactions between LULC trajectories and carbon storage in Sari County, northern Iran. Landsat data for 1995, 2005, and 2025 were classified using the Random Forest algorithm, while future projections to 2060 were generated through the MOLUSCE framework combining Artificial Neural Networks (ANN) and Markov Chains. The results reveal profound transformations characterized by a sixfold expansion of built-up areas, primarily at the expense of cropland (−19%) and forests (−4.3%). This land conversion translated into substantial carbon sequestration losses: areas with negligible carbon stocks (0 Mg C/ha) expanded from ∼7700 ha in 1995 to >26,500 ha by 2060, while high-density carbon pools (235 Mg C/ha) associated with forests declined by more than 7600 ha. These findings highlight urban expansion as the dominant threat to ecosystem carbon balance and climate regulation. Without integrated land-use planning and conservation strategies, the region faces significant reductions in ecosystem services, underscoring the urgent need for sustainable resource management and climate mitigation policies.
