AB  - Real-time ship detection in optical remote sensing images remains challenging due to large scale variation, elongated target geometry, dense ship distribution, weak object boundaries, and complex maritime backgrounds. Although CNN-based detectors provide fast inference, they often have limited capability in modeling long-range contextual dependencies, whereas Transformer-based detectors usually introduce higher computational cost. To address these issues, this paper proposes UMS-DETR, a lightweight end-to-end Transformer-based detector for real-time ship detection in remote sensing images. Built upon the RT-DETR framework, UMS-DETR introduces two task-oriented designs. First, a Multi-Scale Large-Small (MSLS) convolution is designed to enhance ship feature extraction by combining multi-scale large-kernel perception with efficient small-kernel aggregation. Based on this convolutional operator, a lightweight Multi-Scale Large-Small Network (MSLSNet) backbone is developed to extract discriminative multi-scale features while preserving local geometric details of ship targets. Second, a Ship-Aware Dual-Path Agent Fusion (SDAF) module is proposed to enhance high-level semantic representations by integrating efficient global agent-based interaction and local context refinement. In addition, UMS-DETR adopts the uncertainty-minimal query selection strategy from RT-DETR to initialize object queries from reliable and object-relevant feature tokens, thereby improving decoder prediction stability. Extensive experiments are conducted on two public optical remote sensing ship detection benchmarks, ShipRSImageNet and SCCOS. On ShipRSImageNet, UMS-DETR achieves 57.4% mAP, 88.3% mAP50, and 53.4% mAP75 with only 18.1M parameters, 44.5G FLOPs, and 19.1 ms latency. On SCCOS, it obtains 47.5% mAP, 73.4% mAP50, and 41.6% mAP75, outperforming representative CNN-based and Transformer-based detectors. Qualitative visualization and ablation studies further demonstrate that the proposed MSLSNet backbone and SDAF module improve small ship sensitivity, localization accuracy, feature discrimination, and robustness in cluttered maritime scenes.
