AB  - Ocean fronts are sharp physical and biogeochemical interfaces that organize upper-ocean circulation and ecosystems. Mapping them from satellite imagery remains challenging: classical approaches rely on gradient- or statistic-based criteria and can be sensitive to parameter choices and data characteristics, whereas existing deep learning models often struggle to generalize across products and resolutions. We present an uncertainty-aware, self-supervised framework that fuses spatial and frequency cues with pixel-level confidence weighting derived from uncertainties in satellite products. The method avoids manual thresholds, improves weak-front reliability, and preserves continuity for strong and moderate fronts. In cross-product evaluations against annotated fronts and ship-based observations, it achieves higher precision, recall, intersection-over-union and hit rate performance, while reducing spurious detections in low signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) conditions. Applied globally to long-term multi-satellite SST and chlorophyll-a datasets, the framework generates daily thermal (1981–2022, 0.05°) and chlorophyll-a (1997–2022, 4 km) front maps, providing per-pixel location, intensity, and uncertainty. A case study in the eastern tropical Pacific resolves distinct responses of thermal versus chlorophyll-a fronts to tropical instability waves, the chlorophyll-a-rich tongue, and ENSO phases. This resolution-agnostic approach provides a generalizable basis for robust, uncertainty-aware front detection in satellite remote sensing. Code, pretrained weights, and exemplar products are released to support reuse.
