AB  - By reviewing the studies that have been conducted so far in the field of earthquake prediction using satellite data, it can be said that all phases related to the creation of an earthquake warning system have been almost completed. But why has this set of activities not yet led to earthquake prediction with acceptable uncertainty? The role of satellite remote sensing data in the development of earthquake prediction studies is undeniable. Although the improvement in spatial, temporal, spectral, and radiometric resolution and the diversity of satellite sensors have been significant, it does not seem sufficient for earthquake prediction, and the question that arises is whether the process of improving these satellite parameters can lead to earthquake prediction in the future? Regarding the detection of clear and sometimes hidden seismic anomalies in satellite data, existing classical and intelligent algorithms based on machine learning have been effective. But will it be possible to detect anomalies with unknown patterns related to seismic activity in the future by providing appropriate training data and resolving ambiguities related to the mechanisms of occurrence of seismic activity? Will deep learning-based intelligent algorithms be able to estimate earthquake parameters (time, location, and magnitude) with very low uncertainty in the future? In the case of integrating earthquake precursors, or in other words, earthquake parameters estimated by different precursors, existing simple and intelligent fusion algorithms are persuasive. Therefore, it seems that the biggest ambiguity and challenge that has been influential in the implementation process of the proposed earthquake warning systems is the lack of a precise definition of the LAIC (Lithospheric Atmospheric Ionospheric Coupling) mechanism. But the question arises whether effective and accurate earthquake warning systems will be provided in the future by resolving these existing ambiguities? This article reviews associated studies and challenges regarding the use of remote sensing data in earthquake prediction, and proposes a roadmap for the future of these studies.
