AB  - Coastal wetlands are crucial for maintaining the ecological stability of land and sea interfaces. Rapid and precise monitoring wetlands using remote sensing data is of great significance. However, challenges such as difficulties in field data collection often result in limited reliable samples and imbalanced sample categories, which can constrain monitoring accuracy. To address these issues, this study comprehensively evaluates the contribution of sample augmentation under different machine learning classifiers for fine-scale wetland mapping using optical, SAR, and combined data. We also propose the JM distance-supervised automatic sample augmentation method to enhance the reliability of augmented samples. Using China’s Yellow River Delta (YRD) wetland as the study area, experiments were conducted based on GF-3 radar data and Sentinel-2 optical data, combined with feature selection and three machine learning classifiers. The results show that the proposed method achieves the highest accuracy across different feature sets and classifiers, with an OA improvement of 0.11–7.93% compared to methods without sample augmentation, reaching an OA of 92.85%. Our method performs better on combined SAR features, improving wetland classification OA by an average of 3.24% compared with single-modal raw SAR data, likely due to the higher discriminative power of the integrated feature space. In contrast, the OA under optical data increases by an average of 0.92%. These findings provide useful insights into the application of sample augmentation strategies in remote sensing classification and demonstrate the potential applicability of the proposed JM-based framework for wetland mapping under limited-sample conditions. Furthermore, the proposed method represents a promising approach that merits further validation and testing across additional wetland ecosystems and environmental settings.
