AB  - Mining activities play a critical role in industrial and economic development but are also a major driver of land surface degradation, including deforestation, soil erosion, and water contamination. Effective environmental governance therefore requires consistent, long-term monitoring of mining-induced land surface changes, yet existing remote sensing datasets are often limited either in temporal depth or in their focus on mining activities. In this paper, we present EuroMineNet, a multiyear benchmark for mining footprint mapping and change detection based on Sentinel-2 multispectral imagery. EuroMineNet covers 133 mining sites across the European Union and provides annual expert-verified mining footprint annotations from 2015 to 2024, enabling year-by-year analysis of mining activity dynamics at regional scale. The benchmark supports two complementary tasks: (1) multitemporal footprint mapping, which aims to generate temporally consistent annual maps of mining areas, evaluated using a proposed Change-Aware Temporal Intersection-over-Union (CA-TIoU) metric; and (2) cross-temporal change detection, which targets the identification of mining-induced changes across arbitrary temporal intervals. We benchmark 31 state-of-the-art deep learning models on these tasks. Our results show that while current GeoAI methods reliably capture long-term mining expansion, they continue to struggle with detecting subtle short-term changes. By providing a decade-long, consistently annotated dataset and a standardized evaluation protocol, EuroMineNet establishes a reference benchmark for advancing robust and temporally consistent mining footprint mapping and change detection using Earth observation data. The dataset and code are released following FAIR and open science principles at https://github.com/EricYu97/EuroMineNet.
