AB  - As the quality and availability of arable land decline, there is an urgent need to improve land use practices to meet growing food production demands. Improving the utilization of winter fallow arable lands offers a feasible strategy to boost food supply without expanding cultivated areas. However, traditional methods for identifying winter fallow arable lands often struggle to establish suitable thresholds to identify winter fallow arable lands. In addition, previous supervised classification studies have faced challenges in assessing fallow periods. To address these limitations, a framework was developed to identify winter fallow arable lands and fallow duration in Luoyang City for 2020 using Sentinel-2 images. Firstly, two feature combinations were constructed and optimized using the Recursive Feature Elimination (RFE) algorithm, yielding Key Winter Fallow Identification Feature Sets (KWIF1) and (KWIF2). Then, four classification schemes were then developed by integrating Random Forest (RF) and Support Vector Machine (SVM) algorithms to determine the optimal classification scheme. Then the Geospatial Soil Sensing System (GEOS3) method was used to estimate fallow duration based on the classification results. The results revealed that the SVM method outperformed the RF method, and the SVM using KWIF1 yielded the best classification performance. The area classified as winter fallow arable lands accounts for 68.74% of the total cropland area, predominantly in regions with lower elevation and gentle gradients. The spatial distribution indicated that central-eastern and western counties experienced shorter fallow periods in Luoyang City. Notably, 59.54% of fallow land remained fallow beyond 80 days, highlighting significant potential for further development. This study can provide insights into optimizing land use and sustainable agriculture.
