AB  - Multimodal remote sensing combines spectral, scattering, textural, and structural cues to improve mangrove species mapping. However, most evidence for these benefits comes from within-scene evaluations. It remains unclear whether multimodal fusion still helps or hurts generalization under cross-scene discrepancies. To address this gap, we build a cross-scene dataset across three mangrove reserves in southern China with five modalities—multispectral imagery (MSI), synthetic aperture radar (SAR), hyperspectral imagery (HSI), panchromatic imagery (PAN), and canopy height model (CHM)—and define six few-shot transfer tasks. We further propose a Multimodal Contrastive Domain Adaptation Network (MCDAN) that combines Layer Normalization (LN) with supervised contrastive learning to exploit limited target labels under cross-scene distribution shifts. Across five independent runs, MCDAN achieved a mean overall accuracy (OA) of (Formula presented) and a mean intersection over union (mIoU) of (Formula presented) over the six transfer directions. Analysis of all 31 non-empty modality combinations revealed diminishing average marginal gains as additional modalities were introduced. MSI+PAN+CHM attained mean performance close to the full five-modality combination with fewer input modalities, whereas the incremental contribution of SAR varied among scenes, species, and base combinations. These findings provide empirical guidance for modality configuration under the cross-scene mangrove-mapping conditions represented by the three study reserves.
