AB  - Due to spectral mixing and the high similarity of spectral signatures among different land-cover categories, optical remote sensing image classification remains a challenging task. Most existing methods implicitly assign identical attribute scores to all images, failing to capture the unique characteristics of individual sample, which fundamentally limits classification performance. To address this issue, we propose a novel deep feature learning framework embedded with latent relationship penalty term (DFLRPSFS), which explicitly exploits image-specific uniqueness. Specifically, a ResMLP module consisting of multi-layer perceptron (MLP) and residual connections (Res) is integrated into the fine-tuned VGG16 model to enhance the feature representations. Furthermore, we propose latent relationship-preserving sparse feature selection (LRPSFS), which introduces a latent relationship penalty term. The penalty term exploits image-specific uniqueness as prior knowledge to guide the selection process. Finally, the support vector machine (SVM) is employed to generate the classification results. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed DFLRPSFS outperforms state-of-the-art methods, achieving accuracy improvements of 7.54% on the UCM dataset, 8.93% and 8.64% on 20% and 50% AID30 subsets, and 9.97% and 11.44% on 10% and 20% NWPU-RESISC45 subsets, respectively, validating its effectiveness and superiority.
