AB  - Road extraction from high resolution remote sensing images is a significant research area in computer vision. However, the sparse pixel distribution of fine road structures often leads to severe degradation of local features during the successive downsampling operations in conventional convolutional neural networks, making it challenging to preserve the pixel-level continuity and completeness of roads. To address these issues, we propose a Dynamic Sparse Enhancement Network (DSENet) that effectively focuses on fine road segments. Specifically, the Dynamic Sparse Enhancement Module (DSEM) is proposed to mitigate the discontinuous extraction of fine roads by estimating multi-scale spatial scores, selecting grid-constrained Top-K localized windows, and enhancing sparse fine-road features within these windows. Furthermore, leveraging the elongated geometric prior of roads, we introduce orthogonal strip convolution in the encoder to better capture long-range contextual information along horizontal and vertical directions, thereby improving road recognition accuracy. Extensive experiments on DeepGlobe and CHN6-CUG datasets demonstrate that DSENet achieves IoU scores of 65.91% and 67.65%, respectively, and outperforms the compared representative road extraction methods, achieving superior accuracy, completeness, and pixel-level continuity in road extraction.
ER  -

