N1  - Export Date: 05 October 2026
N1  - Cited By: 0
AB  - This study presents a machine learning-based framework to improve water pixel detection in SWOT Pixel Cloud (PIXC) data for reservoir monitoring applications. Unlike general land-cover classification, the primary objective is to reduce false positive and false negative water detections in PIXC products, thereby improving the accuracy of derived reservoir surface area and volume estimates.The proposed framework integrates PIXC-derived features with an external long-term water occurrence prior (based on Landsat 5–8 observations) to refine pixel-level water detection. Three supervised learning algorithms, namely K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN), Random Forest (RF), and Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), are used not only as predictive models but also as diagnostic tools to investigate the behavior of PIXC-derived inputs within the feature space. These inputs include radar backscatter observables, interferometric coherence, detection quality indicators, and geometry-dependent parameters.The models are trained and evaluated across three reservoirs in Iran (Dez, Shahid Rajaei, and Agh Chay), selected to represent distinct hydro-climatic and geomorphological conditions, and are subsequently applied to eight reservoirs to assess their spatial transferability. Performance is quantified using pixel-level Intersection-over-Union (IoU), together with hydrological consistency metrics including correlation coefficient, Normalized Root Mean Square Error (NRMSE), and Kling–Gupta Efficiency (KGE) against in-situ observations.Beyond predictive performance, the results provide interpretability insights into PIXC input features. In particular, features related to detection uncertainty, interferometric coherence, and imaging geometry consistently show strong influence on water detection performance, suggesting that these components of the PIXC processing chain play a critical role in misclassification patterns. This finding highlights potential directions for improving uncertainty representation and quality control within future SWOT PIXC products.Results indicate that all machine learning models improve agreement with both SWOT PIXC and LakeSP products in terms of water detection performance and consistency with in-situ-derived reservoir area estimates.Overall, the findings suggest that incorporating physically meaningful PIXC features together with external hydrological priors can significantly enhance water pixel detection reliability. This approach provides practical guidance for improving downstream SWOT-derived reservoir products and offers insights that may support future refinements of the PIXC processing chain without requiring modifications to the original satellite data stream.
ER  -

