M3  - Review
DB  - Scopus
N1  - EID: 2-s2.0-105037518627
N1  - Export Date: 05 October 2026
N1  - Cited By: 0
AB  - Significance Hyperspectral imaging (HSI), by acquiring hundreds of narrow contiguous spectral bands from samples, has invited broad applications spanning from remote sensing to biomedical diagnosis. Unlike conventional RGB imaging, HSI generates a three-dimensional data cube comprising spatial (x, y) and spectral (λ) information, enabling label-free and non-invasive characterization of sample composition, molecular structure, and micro-morphology. The unique feature of combining imaging and spectroscopy renders HSI suited for early disease detection, precise pathological differentiation, and quantitative surgical guidance. However, the intrinsic high dimensionality, spectral redundancy, and large data volume of hyperspectral data pose substantial analytical challenges, often referred to as the "curse of dimensionality" for traditional HSI methods. Deep learning (DL), with its hierarchical and automated feature extraction capability, offers an effective solution for HSI data analysis, i.e., mining joint spatial–spectral information. Hence, the integration of HSI and DL represents a crucial pathway toward intelligent, objective, and automated inspection in many fields. Within this review, we focus on the deep learning-integrated hyperspectral imaging (DL-HSI) for biomedical applications. Progress DL-HSI acquires spectral–spatial data cubes with HSI platforms and perform data mining using deep learning. The typical HSI platforms are primarily categorized into four imaging modalities: whiskbroom, pushbroom, staring, and snapshot imaging (Fig.1). These modalities represent different trade-offs between spectral resolution, spatial resolution, acquisition speed, and hardware complexity, allowing HSI systems to adapt to diverse biomedical scenarios ranging from high-precision ex vivo tissue analysis to real-time intraoperative imaging. Meanwhile, for intelligent data mining, four representative deep learning frameworks have been widely adopted for hyperspectral data analysis (Fig.2). Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) extract local spatial–spectral features and serve as the foundation for pixel-level classification and segmentation. Residual Networks (ResNets) adopt very deep architectures and is well suited for learning highly discriminative pathological representations. Transformer architectures capture long-range dependencies across spectral bands and spatial regions through self-attention mechanisms. Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) address practical challenges such as limited annotated samples, super-resolution reconstruction, and domain adaptation. The global comparison of these four classical DL frameworks on the core ideas, key advantages and limitations, and application scenarios is summarized in Tab.1, serving as a practical guide for researchers to analyze and select appropriate models. Figure 3 illustrates the data processing workflow of deep learning-integrated hyperspectral imaging (DL-HSI) technology, showing how spectral–spatial data cubes are acquired and processed by deep architectures to yield discriminative diagnostic features. Advances in both imaging systems and algorithms have driven significant progress of DL-HSI, and the representative applications of DL-HSI are elaborated in the following four aspects. In oncology, DL-HSI has demonstrated high-accuracy in tumor identification, classification, and margin delineation across multiple cancer types, with visualization techniques revealing discriminative spectral regions and validating model interpretability (Fig.4-Fig.5). In ophthalmology, DL-HSI imaging of fundus enables label-free detection of retinal diseases, super-resolution reconstruction using snapshot systems, and quantitative assessment of retinal vascular characteristics and oxygen saturation (Fig.6-Fig.7). In cardiovascular and neurological applications, DL-HSI has been successfully applied to ablated atrial tissue classification, brain tumor or vessel segmentation, and tissue characterization under limited annotation conditions, demonstrating its feasibility for intraoperative and functional assessment (Fig.8-Fig.9). Beyond the above applications, additional applications include burn wound assessment, diabetic foot monitoring, atopic dermatitis evaluation, and other non-invasive tissue analysis tasks, highlighting the broad versatility of this technology (Fig.10-Fig.11). Conclusions and Prospects DL-HSI that integrates hyperspectral imaging and deep learning represents a paradigm shift in biomedical diagnostics by acquiring spectral–spatial information and transforming the high-dimensional data into clinically actionable insights. This review systematically summarizes the underlying imaging principles of HSI systems and core algorithmic frameworks of DL-based data analysis, and representative biomedical applications across multiple disease domains. Future clinical translation will be pushed forward with the standardization of hyperspectral data acquisition and joint-utilization of public spectral databases, miniaturization and portability of HSI hardware for point-of-care and intraoperative use, development of real-time processing algorithms, and improved model interpretability for clinical trust. With continued advances in hardware and algorithm design, DL-HSI technology is expected to become an essential tool for non-invasive screening, real-time monitoring, and precision medicine across an expanding range of medical specialties.
