AB  - Deep learning (DL) has become the foundation of contemporary Land Cover and Crop Type (LCCT) classification practices, yet its performance largely depends on the availability of large spatial patches (e.g., 512 × 512 or 256 × 256 pixels) that offer rich contextual information. However, in practice, obtaining such large spatial contexts in topographically complex regions is often challenging, as it is both costly and labor-intensive. At the same time, valuable freely available pixel-point reference data, collected through field surveys or expert interpretation, remain underutilized to train modern DL models because they lack rich contextual information. To address this gap, this study employs a simple yet effective method that centers small image patches on reference sample pixels, producing 9 × 9 pixel inputs. We then, utilizing Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 data as input data, systematically evaluated the performance of five widely used DL architectures in LCCT classification (U-Net, U-Net++, Res-U-Net++, DeepLabV3+, and DCNN) when trained on these very small patches across a topographically complex region. Our assessment showed that, across the 14 LCCT classes, U-Net++ achieved the highest classification performance (75.45% overall accuracy), followed by DeepLabV3+ (70.86%), while DCNN produced the lowest accuracy (62.12%). We found that models with nested skip connections and enhanced feature reuse exhibited greater robustness to the loss of spatial context and better extraction of fine-scale patterns. This research discusses the limits and potential of DL architectures under small patch-size constraints and offers practical guidance for data-efficient LCCT mapping in data-limited or heterogeneous regions.
