AB  - Forest canopy height (FCH) and vertical structural composition are essential for urban ecological assessment and precision forest management, yet reliable retrieval in fragmented urban–rural transition landscapes remains challenging due to multi-source sensor heterogeneity, seasonal dynamics, and mixed canopy conditions within 10 m pixels. We propose MiMNet-STASF, a spatiotemporal fusion framework for grid-level canopy-structure inversion from 24-month Sentinel-1/2 time series. The framework integrates a nested Mamba architecture (Inner VMamba for multi-scale spatial encoding and Outer bidirectional Mamba for long-range temporal dependency modeling), an adaptive Spatiotemporal Arbiter Gated Fusion (STASF) module (SG/TG/STAG), and a physics-informed hierarchical regression head that jointly estimates FCH and vertical structural fractions (<10 m, 10–20 m, and ≥20 m). Using UAV LiDAR-derived supervision, validated in Xiong’an New Area against 13 deep learning models spanning four architectural paradigms (RNNs, CNNs, Transformers, SSMs), MiMNet-STASF outperforms all comparatives, achieving an R2 of 0.8963 and an RMSE of 1.0903 m for FCH retrieval. Ablation results indicate that the nested Mamba and STASF components provide clearer gains on structurally imbalanced targets. From a data perspective, Sentinel-1 provides enhanced structural sensitivity, Sentinel-2 offers stable spectral and phenological context, and vegetation indices (VI) further improve vegetation contrast; their fusion enhances prediction consistency across canopy-height gradients and vertical fractional components. Moreover, MiMNet-STASF retains robust structural representations under seasonal vegetation variation. Together with cross-regional GEDI-referenced evaluation, these results support MiMNet-STASF as an effective and scalable solution for management-oriented, fine-grained urban forest canopy-structure mapping. The implementation of the proposed method is publicly available at https://github.com/qinjs123/MiMNet-STASF.
