AB  - As strategic national mineral resources, the accurate prediction and exploration of rare Earths are critically important. This article introduces rare-Earth prospect area prediction network combining cross-layer dilated features and attention-based convolutional networks (RCACNet), a novel convolutional neural network model designed to address the challenges of target spectral similarity and scale variability in predicting ion-adsorption rare-Earth deposits from remote sensing imagery. At its core, the model’s effectiveness stems from a synergistic mechanism between a hierarchical cross-level feature fusion (CFF) path and a dense atrous spatial pyramid pooling (DenseASPP) module. This design improves the conventional encoder–decoder structure by utilizing the CFF-refined features as enhanced skip connections for the decoder. This ensures that features rich in spatial detail are semantically purified before being used for reconstruction. Meanwhile, the DenseASPP module captures rich multiscale context, ensuring a comprehensive feature representation that drives the final prediction. Furthermore, integrated attention mechanisms facilitate adaptive feature refinement by emphasizing critical spectral and spatial discriminative features during extraction. This collectively enhances the model’s capability to resolve mineralization targets within spectrally similar backgrounds. A dataset was constructed using remote sensing images from ion-adsorption rare-Earth mining areas in typical hilly regions of southern China. Comprehensive comparative experiments were conducted against established semantic segmentation networks, including U-Net, DeepLabV3+, HRNet, and PSPNet, as well as a vision transformer-based model, to validate the effectiveness and superiority of RCACNet systematically. The results indicate that RCACNet attained optimal performance across all key evaluation metrics, demonstrating significant improvements. Compared with baseline models, it achieved an 18.2% increase in mean intersection over union and a 17.49% increase in macro-F1-score. Moreover, large-scale prediction experiments utilizing overlapping tile inference with an edge-weighted averaging strategy visually demonstrated that RCACNet generates high-quality prediction maps featuring sharp boundaries, internal integrity, and superior spatial consistency compared with the outputs of other models. These findings collectively underscore the exceptional performance and considerable application potential of RCACNet for ion-adsorption rare-Earth mineralization target prediction.
