AB  - The widely used traditional approaches to landslide prediction lack accuracy and adaptability due to their reliance on data from single sources in mountainous and monsoon-prone areas. This paper presents the Smart Multi-Data Landslide Prediction System (SMLPS) to improve landslide forecasting through an optimized deep learning framework that integrates satellite imagery, weather parameters, and soil characteristics. The system employs hybrid architecture where CNNs extract spatial features and LSTM networks with an attention mechanism model temporal dependency. Ensemble fusion of CNN and LSTM-attention outputs enhanced robustness, resulting in 92.5% accuracy with an F1-score of 0.89, outperforming the single LSTM model (F1-score 0.74). Bayesian Neural Networks quantify predictive reliability with a classification confidence of 90.1% on average. The results prove the efficiency of SMLPS as a reliable, multi-source, and uncertainty-aware landslide prediction system to support timely decision-making and enhance disaster risk management.
