AB  - Accurate evapotranspiration (ET) estimation remains challenging because physically based formulas rely on simplifying assumptions, whereas satellite products contain retrieval uncertainty. We develop a multi-modal dual-encoder, dual-target spatio-temporal graph neural network for regional ET estimation over the Korean Peninsula. The framework treats meteorological forcing and MODIS-derived surface descriptors as heterogeneous but complementary modes. A hybrid graph represents geographic-distance and inter-station-correlation structure, modality-specific graph encoders and a recurrent temporal encoder capture spatio-temporal dependence, and a learnable gate fuses climate and satellite representations while quantifying their relative contributions. The model is trained jointly against MODIS ET and FAO-56 Penman–Monteith reference ET (PM ET0), combining satellite-observed actual ET with meteorological evaporative demand. On the holdout test set, the model achieved MAE/RMSE/R2 of 2.197/3.503/0.883 for MODIS ET and 0.473/0.615/0.999 for PM ET0. Relative to a graph-free CNN + LSTM dual-head baseline, MAE decreased by 33.3% for MODIS ET and 39.0% for PM ET0. Component ablation showed that the full multi-modal dual-target configuration reduced joint MAE by 40.0% and RMSE by 38.6% relative to the single-encoder MODIS variant. Gate analysis showed that climate and satellite contributions vary by season and input position, making the fusion interpretable rather than fixed. MC-dropout uncertainty with isotonic calibration achieved PICP90 of 0.895 and 0.899 for MODIS ET and PM ET0, respectively. Multi-horizon tests retained seasonal predictability up to 12 months.
