AB  - Rapid urbanization alters land use and land cover (LULC), increasing landscape fragmentation and intensifying land surface temperature (LST), thereby posing significant challenges for sustainable urban planning. This study aims to systematically quantify urban expansion, assess its morphological and thermal impacts, and forecast future LST dynamics in Lucknow, India, from 1991 to 2021. Using multi-decadal Landsat data, we developed year-specific Random Forest (RF) classifiers trained on 17 features including six spectral bands (Blue, Green, Red, NIR, SWIR1, SWIR2), three indices (NDVI, NDBI, NDWI), and eight GLCM texture metrics extracted from NIR and SWIR1 bands. Spatial autocorrelation of training samples was evaluated using Moran’s I (0.35–0.48), and mitigated through spatial separation and cross-validated grid search optimization. LULC models achieved high accuracy with test accuracies of 88.00 % (1991), 86.12 % (2001), 88.13 % (2011), and 87.67 % (2021), and kappa values ranging from 0.8562 to 0.8741. LST was retrieved using the Mono-Window Algorithm (MWA), revealing a mean LST increase of ∼2.5 °C per decade, with 55 % of the region exceeding 30 °C by 2021. Morphological Spatial Pattern Analysis (MSPA) identified growing fragmentation in agricultural and open land (e.g., loss of core and bridge elements) and densification in built-up zones. Polynomial regression models (degrees 2–4) were used to forecast LST for 2031 across MSPA classes, predicting highest values in built-up cores (34.30 °C) and bridges (32.51 °C), and lowest in core agriculture (25.77 °C). These findings confirm that landscape form and composition strongly regulate urban thermal patterns. The study presents an integrated modeling pipeline linking machine learning classification, thermal retrieval, and morphological analysis to anticipate urban heat trajectories, providing critical insights for climate-adaptive land use planning and resilient urban design.
