AB  - Accurate determination of iron-ore grades enables optimized post-processing workflows, reducing energy consumption and minimizing pollution emissions. However, when identifying ore grades by relying on a limited number of features in hyperspectral images (HSI), existing methods suffered from poor separability between certain categories and did not account for adaptability across different mining areas. To better determine the grades of iron ores from HSI, this paper proposed a method to enhance grade separability of ores collected from different mines. First, spectral bands sensitive to ore grades were extracted through feature selection method of SPA, thereby reducing redundant information. The Gramian Angular Difference Field (GADF) transformation was then employed to convert the one-dimensional spectral data into two-dimensional image representations, leveraging more stable relationships between the different bands of the iron ores. To further capture spatial structures contained within the GADF features, a deep learning network structure was designed to classify and identify ore grades, which incorporates multi-scale convolution kernels and a feature layer weighting strategy. The experiment results indicated that the GADF transform with 15 representative bands improved the grade separability to 1.81 and deep learning further improved classification accuracy exceeding 2.7%. Classification accuracy of validation across four mines of Tangshan, Shougang, Handan and Yantai reached 95.88%, 84.65%, 85.18% and 92.12%, respectively. The proposed data processing framework demonstrated effective performance across a diverse range of ore samples, indicating its practical application potential in identification and production processes.
