AB  - Few-shot remote sensing image scene classification (FS-RSI-SC) has emerged as a promising approach to alleviate the large-scale data reliance in the scene classification task and has garnered increasing attention. However, most existing FS-RSI-SC methods require a base training stage with large amounts of relevant remote sensing scene images before adapting to the few-shot novel scene category. Recently, Visual-Language Models (VLMs) have demonstrated remarkable cross-domain transferability and enabled rapid adaptation to downstream tasks with only a few annotated samples, providing a novel insight into the FS-RSI-SC task. Therefore, in this paper, we concentrate on effectively adapting the pre-trained VLMs to the FS-RSI-SC task and propose a novel Multi-granularity Prompt Learning Network (MPLNet). Considering that scene categories contain multi-granularity information about ground objects, we propose a Multi-granularity Visual Representations (MVR) module to capture scene information from global, regional, and local granularities. Next, we design a Multi-granularity Textual Representations (MTR) module that learns diverse textual representations tailored to different granularity visual representations. Furthermore, an Attention-guided Similarity Mechanism (ASM) is presented to highlight the scene-relevant semantic information and alleviate the background noise interference. Comprehensive experiments on four FS-RSI-SC datasets demonstrate that our proposed MPLNet achieves the state-of-the-art classification accuracy across all few-shot settings. The code is available at https://github.com/Trinstar/MPLNet.
