AB  - Snow avalanche debris mapping is critical for hazard assessment in high-altitude terrain, yet remains challenging using satellite imagery due to spectral similarity with surrounding snow and terrain-induced illumination effects. This study presents the first framework for automated avalanche debris detection using Sentinel-2 imagery combining multi-index spectral fusion with a stepwise-optimised deep learning model having strong potential for practical application. An 8-channel input is constructed by combining multispectral bands with spectral indices (NDSI, NDWI, NDVI). A U-Net model is then trained using a structured, stepwise hyperparameter optimization strategy to isolate the effects of loss function, optimizer, batch size, and patch size. Class imbalance and heterogeneous scene conditions present significant challenges for avalanche debris segmentation. Tversky loss with asymmetric weighting best handled class imbalance, outperforming binary cross-entropy and Dice loss. AdamW was selected as the optimal optimizer based on both quantitative performance and qualitative robustness, demonstrating fewer false alarms than Nadam. A batch size of 16 and a patch size of 128 × 128 were found to be optimal, demonstrating superior generalization (Validation Dice = 0.658) compared to other configurations. On an independent test set with scenes spanning different dates, the final configuration achieved a mean Dice of 0.681 ± 0.054. Adding spectral indices improved performance over bands-only input across all metrics, Dice rose from 0.640 ± 0.040 to 0.681 ± 0.054, IoU from 0.473 ± 0.038 to 0.518 ± 0.059, precision from 0.653 to 0.707, recall from 0.643 to 0.663, and MCC from 0.637 to 0.676, confirming improved class separability with multi-index fusion. The optimised framework was validated on two temporally independent Sentinel-2 scenes, with cross-sensor validation against coincident PlanetScope imagery confirming spatial accuracy across sub-regions, terrain types, and deposit scales with an object-based overlap comparison yielding Precision = 0.61, Recall = 0.87, and F1 = 0.72. The proposed framework demonstrates consistent performance across tested scenes without scene-specific parameter tuning. The key contribution lies in the systematic combination of open-access Sentinel-2 imagery, multi-index spectral fusion, stepwise optimization, and cross-sensor validation, which together yield a robust, scalable framework for segmenting low-contrast cryospheric features in rugged, difficult terrain where limited field accessibility constrains conventional ground-based assessment.
