AB  - Under Antarctica's highly complex climatic conditions, characterizing building-relevant meteorological variability and constructing typical meteorological year (TMY) datasets for distinct climate zones are essential for climate-adaptive research stations. However, existing TMY methods rely on fixed weighting schemes and cannot capture the nonstationary spatiotemporal variability of Antarctic meteorological parameters or the nonlinear coupling among climatic factors affecting building loads, leading to deviations in building performance simulations. To address this issue, a continuous meteorological dataset was developed from PANDA observations supplemented with satellite and reanalysis products. Three building climate zones are identified, including plateau extreme cold, steep-slope extreme wind, and coastal severe cold zones, and five representative stations are selected. A building-response-informed weighting scheme based on long short-term memory (LSTM) is introduced to replace the fixed empirical weights in the Sandia method. Results show that temperature is the most influential meteorological parameter. Compared with the fixed temperature weight of 0.2 in the conventional Sandia method, its weight increases to approximately 0.6 in the plateau extreme cold zone, reducing the mean absolute error (MAE) of building energy simulation by 0.2 kWh/m2. Compared with conventional methods used in low- and mid-latitude regions, the proposed approach yields TMY results that better match long-term meteorological records and multi-year average building energy consumption, with maximum root mean square error (RMSE) reductions of 3.08 °C and 0.96 kWh/m2. The Sandia-LSTM method provides a building-response-informed approach to weighting-factor determination for TMY generation in extreme climates and supplies representative annual weather inputs for Antarctic building-energy assessment.
