AB  - This study addresses automatic detection of ship wakes in optical remote-sensing images, aiming to improve accuracy and efficiency via joint detection of ships and Kelvin wakes. First, we construct a new Remote Sensing Ship Wake (RSSW) dataset from multi-source optical imagery and develop dedicated labeling software to ensure reliable data preparation. Second, we propose the Discrete Hartley Transform Ship Wake Network (DHT-SWNet), which integrates DHT-based frequency analysis to capture the characteristic patterns of Kelvin wake. To further enhance representation, the network adopts efficient multi-scale feature integration, strengthening learning across different spatial scales. Experimental results show that the method achieves 75.63% mAP for wake detection, outperforming representative deep-learning baselines. The system simultaneously localizes ships and wakes on wide-area optical images, providing an effective tool for maritime surveillance, environmental protection, and ship navigation, and supporting subsequent ocean monitoring and analysis.
