AB  - Significant wave height (SWH) is a crucial parameter in marine environmental monitoring, with substantial implications for navigation, fisheries, and meteorological forecasting. However, traditional methods often struggle with the multifaceted nature of oceanic processes, atmospheric interference, and other factors, making it difficult to measure SWHs accurately. This study developed an efficient ocean wave height monitoring method designed to generate high-precision wave height products for the north-western Pacific Ocean rapidly, based on geostationary satellite data. We employed the CoordConvUnet model, which integrates geographic coordinate information and multi-scale features from Himawari-8 satellite cloud images, and achieved large-scale, high-precision retrieval of SWHs over the north-western Pacific Ocean. The incorporation of pre-training and fine-tuning strategies significantly enhanced the accuracy and stability of the SWH retrievals. Experimental results demonstrated that the proposed deep learning-based approach provided better accuracy than traditional methods, rapidly obtaining SWH retrievals across a 0.5° wave field in the north-western Pacific Ocean (0-60°N, 100-160°E). The mean absolute error for SWH retrievals at the test site was 0.341 m, and the root mean square error was 0.491 m. These results surpassed the accuracy of the HY-2B radar altimeter C-band SWH product. Compared to HY-2B polar orbit satellite data, the data produced by our model show significantly enhanced continuity and coverage, and overcome the limitations associated with traditional polar orbit satellite data, which include an extended acquisition period and sparse coverage. These advancements represent a new paradigm in ocean monitoring that will facilitate more timely marine disaster warnings and more effective marine resource management.
