AB  - Significant advancements have been made in the extraction of high-resolution remote sensing imagery for urban road detection using deep learning. However, the accuracy of this extraction is still subject to various limitations. First, substantial differences between roads lead to the loss of target contextual information due to standard convolution. Second, the lack of distinct differences between roads and their surrounding environments affects the network's judgment of road boundaries. Third, occluded parts of roads, such as those obscured by trees, are challenging to extract. The present study proposes a deep learning network based on the U-shaped Network (UNet) architecture. Specifically, a multi-scale aggregation module is employed to preserve target contextual information, and a self-attention segmentation module is integrated to reinforce boundary delineation and prevent overfitting between targets and contextual information. A graph convolutional module is ultimately employed for the purpose of global association integration. Specifically, the multi-scale aggregation module effectively integrates information from convolutional kernels of various scales and images of different scales. The graph convolutional module employs the Patch Merging approach to reduce the size of feature maps, thereby obtaining global association information. This approach significantly reduces computational load while ensuring accuracy. The self-attention segmentation module, based on the Sobel operator, strengthens the delineation between targets and their surrounding environments. The proposed modules collectively enhance the Intersection over Union (IoU), F1 score, and Precision by 6.9 %, 5.81 %, and 10.8 %, respectively, in the Massachusetts Roads Dataset. The model as a whole achieves a Mean IoU (MIoU) and F1 score of 76.161 %,73.08 %,76.760 %,76.88 % and 77.262 %,76.00 % in urban road datasets such as Massachusetts Roads Dataset, DeepGlobe Roads Dataset, and Self-Constructed Roads Dataset; in the complex mountainous roads of the Mountain Roads Dataset, the model achieves an MIoU and F1 score of 60.980 % and 41.73 %, respectively.
