AB  - Remote sensing plays a crucial role in identifying granite–pegmatite-type lithium deposits during mineral exploration. However, challenges such as ambiguous boundaries, complex textures, and long-range spatial dependencies hinder accurate segmentation of geological features in remote sensing imagery. To address these limitations, a model is proposed that builds on the traditional UNet architecture by introducing a Transformer module, thereby enhancing global feature-capturing capability. The model further integrates a squeeze-and-excitation (SE) channel attention mechanism to enable adaptive enhancement of key feature channels. Experiments were conducted on a dataset constructed from China’s GF-5 satellite imagery. This synergistic design allows the model to capture both long-range dependencies and discriminative local features, significantly improving segmentation accuracy. Results show that SETransformUNet outperforms the traditional UNet, achieving improvements of 9.99% in mIoU and 10.80% in F1-score. The model demonstrates superior performance in preserving boundary continuity and fine structural details, effectively mitigating fragmentation and misclassification in complex geological settings. Notably, the SE module introduces minimal additional parameters and computational overhead, ensuring high efficiency. By balancing accuracy and practicality, SETransformUNet provides a robust solution for automated geological feature extraction. These findings advance intelligent remote sensing interpretation and offer technical support for large-scale digital geological mapping and mineral prospectivity prediction.
