AB  - The development of remote sensing technologies in recent years has led to significant advances in a range of tasks previously solved using more time- and labor-intensive approaches. Rapid monitoring and precise analysis are key goals that are pursued by companies and researchers, driving the progress in geospatial monitoring. In this field, artificial intelligence (AI) technologies serve as a vital instrument to address limitations caused by satellite payload constraints and various sources of signal distortion. AI algorithms can be used to artificially enhance images through denoising, deblurring, and detail improvement. A popular computer vision task for image enhancement is image super-resolution (SR), where an algorithm reconstructs fine-grained details from a lower-resolution image to produce an output equivalent to a higher spatial resolution. The goal of such approaches is to delineate objects and structures that cannot be identified directly in the original imagery. The most common approach in both remote sensing and general computer vision is single-image super-resolution (SISR). In contrast, multi-image super-resolution (MISR) — which utilizes several images from different dates or observation points — has been less thoroughly studied. MISR algorithms strive to capture multiple significant features both in temporal and spatial contexts. Despite the potential of the MISR technique, comprehensive analysis of its methodologies, limitations, and real-world efficacy in the remote sensing domain have not yet been well-studied in the literature. Therefore, this review aims to provide in-depth analysis of MISR techniques, including generative adversarial networks (GANs), visual transformers, and diffusion-based models, applied to remote sensing data. This review proposes a taxonomy and discuss the current main limitations and future development directions in this field with specific respect to satellite imaging, effective evaluation metrics, and downstream tasks.
ER  -

