AB  - Snow algae are crucial terrestrial carbon sinks and light-absorbing impurities in Antarctica. However, the distribution of snow algae across the West Antarctic Peninsula (WAP) remains poorly understood due to their high spatiotemporal variability and complex polar imaging conditions. This study develops a robust, large-scale mapping framework for green and red snow algae using machine learning. Our approach outperformed traditional spectral-index and spectral-unmixing methods across multiple challenging scenes, with the Kappa score improving from 0.51 and 0.49 to 0.82. By synthesizing 2676 Sentinel-2 images, the 2019–2024 composite distribution of WAP snow algae and the interannual variability of bloom intensity were assessed. Results reveal a total bloom area of 54.34 km2, and uncover significant negative association between bloom intensity and the Antarctic Oscillation index. This research establishes a novel, transferable tool for mapping snow algae in cryosphere systems and provides a baseline for evaluating their ecological implications in a changing climate.
