AB  - Spatial resolution of a hyperspectral image (HSI) significantly affects the classification accuracy. Coarse resolution results in mixed pixels that reduce discriminability between classes. In this work, the super-resolution generative adversarial network (SRGAN) model is used to enhance the resolution of HSI. SRGAN generates shaper images from low-resolution input that look perceptually similar to real high-resolution images by enhancing textures and edges. A dual-branch HSI classification framework SRGAN-DCCNN is proposed that combines the spatial features produced by SRGAN with the spectral information through a dual-channel deep convolutional neural network (DCCNN). DCCNN allows independent handling and integration of complementary information leading to better generalization to unseen data. Experiments are carried out on five benchmark datasets including Botswana, Kennedy Space Center (KSC), Pavia University (PaviaU), WHU-Hi-LongKou (WHU), and Salinas. The proposed method achieves a good classification accuracy of 99.29 % ((Formula presented) = 99.23 %), 99.12 % ((Formula presented) = 99.03 %), 99.86 % ((Formula presented) = 99.39 %), 99.76 % ((Formula presented) = 99.67 %), 99.75 % ((Formula presented) = 99.62 %) respectively for the five images. It exhibits good robustness and generalization capabilities for both coarser images such as Botswana and KSC and fine-resolution images such as PaviaU and WHU. The proposed method performed better than several state-of-the-art methods and demonstrates a balanced tradeoff between complexity and performance.
