AB  - The accurate extraction of Agricultural Parcels (AP) from high-resolution remote sensing images is pivotal for advancing agricultural digitization. However, existing deep learning methods encounter significant challenges when dealing with multi-source and heterogeneous agricultural landscapes. Specifically, the substantial variations in parcel scales and interference from complex background textures compromise model generalization. Furthermore, current boundary regression approaches lack effective uncertainty modeling mechanisms to address label noise and blurred boundaries. Moreover, while emerging geospatial foundation models demonstrate superior performance, their prohibitive computational costs and deficiency in domain-specific semantic constraints hinder their scalable application in agricultural monitoring. To address these issues, we first constructed the Multi-Regional Agricultural Parcels (MR-AP) benchmark dataset, covering seven typical agricultural regions in China. We then proposed MTFormer, an AP extraction model that unites the Uncertainty-Aware Abrupt-Change Distance Transform (UADT) with a Multi-task Transformer. Specifically, MTFormer employs a multi-scale Transformer fusion module to parallelly capture global semantic dependencies and local features. Innovatively, we replaced the standard Euclidean distance with UADT, transforming the regression task into interpretable soft-label. supervision to mitigate label noise and blurred boundaries. Additionally, a multi-task joint deep supervision strategy was introduced to collaboratively optimize mask, boundary, and regression tasks. Experimental results demonstrate that MTFormer outperforms 10 mainstream segmentation models on the MR-AP dataset, achieving a Precision, Completeness, and R2 of 94.08%, 83.93%, and 0.772, respectively. In zero-shot transfer tests across six public datasets, its Precision consistently remained above 90%, verifying the model’s robust cross-scene generalization capability. Further quantitative analysis of landscape heterogeneity reveals that, when confronted with highly complex and fragmented landscapes, geospatial foundation models are highly prone to severe over-segmentation due to a lack of AP priors. In contrast, MTFormer, leveraging the anti-interference capability of its proprietary architecture, consistently maintains highly stable extraction accuracy. Additionally, MTFormer achieves a superior trade-off between accuracy and efficiency, making it highly suitable for practical deployment and scalable application in agricultural monitoring.
ER  -

