N1  - Export Date: 05 October 2026
N1  - Cited By: 0
AB  - Remote sensing disaster impact understanding aims to rapidly identify key affected objects, determine damage severity, and generate effective semantic representations from pre- and post-disaster imagery, thereby providing essential support for emergency response and post-disaster assessment. However, most existing methods are designed for a single task and generally rely on a static and uniform paradigm of global bi-temporal comparison, which limits their ability to effectively model the critical cues in disaster scenes. To address this issue, we propose BlinkChange, an active perception-driven framework for reliable bi-temporal representation learning toward remote sensing disaster impact understanding. Inspired by the human visual system, which understands complex scenes through a sequential, blink-like focus on salient regions, the proposed framework introduces learnable perception frames into a unified video-modeling backbone, transforming bi-temporal modeling from passive discrepancy comparison into a stage-wise representation optimization process driven by key disaster-affected regions, and providing a reliable representational foundation for downstream disaster understanding tasks. Specifically, we first design a change-aware discrepancy construction module (CADC) to generate change context with stronger disaster-semantic discriminability in a content-aware manner. On this basis, we further propose a saliency-guided dynamic perception enhancement strategy (SDPE), which converts spatial saliency into perception-level importance allocation. This is followed by a soft enhancement–suppression updating mechanism (SEU) that continuously strengthens critical disaster cues while suppressing redundant responses during progressive encoding, enabling the model to gradually shift its representational focus from global scene correspondence to the key disaster-affected regions that truly determine task decisions. Extensive experiments demonstrate that the proposed approach achieves state-of-the-art (SOTA) image captioning performance on two recent large-scale disaster understanding datasets, RSCC and DisasterM3, and also delivers strong performance on the widely used building damage assessment dataset xBD with relatively low model complexity. Further ablation studies, visualizations, and external-scene generalization experiments verify the effectiveness of each component and show that the proposed method learns bi-temporal representations that are more sensitive to fine-grained damage cues and more robust to complex background interference. The code is released at https://github.com/ChenHuyoo.
ER  -

