AB  - Hyperspectral imaging (HSI) technology has significant engineering application potential as an innovative method for detecting contamination levels on high-voltage insulators. However, current research primarily focuses on laboratory conditions, and there is a notable lack of studies addressing the direct and effective outdoor detection of insulator contamination. Additionally, practical engineering application demands lightweight detection models that balance high accuracy with rapid inference speed and low computational complexity. Motivated by these challenges, this paper first investigates and confirms the feasibility of directly detecting outdoor contamination levels on insulators using hyperspectral imagery. Then, a novel Fast Contamination Recognition Network (FCRN) is proposed, capable of rapidly and accurately identifying the contamination levels from hyperspectral images collected under realistic outdoor conditions. The FCRN leverages an advanced knowledge distillation framework, compressing the powerful yet complex Spatial-Spectral Pyramid Network (SSPN), initially designed for hyperspectral classification, into a lightweight convolutional neural network. This compression significantly reduces computational overhead while preserving the superior spectral-spatial feature extraction capability of the larger model. Experimental results demonstrate that most tested models achieved over 80% accuracy across outdoor contamination insulators datasets, strongly confirming the practicality and robustness of outdoor hyperspectral-based contamination detection. Notably, the FCRN not only dramatically reduces the computational overhead of the model (more than 90% reduction in the number of parameters, memory, and FLOPs), but also effectively improves the accuracy of the lightweight model by 1–2%, successfully narrowing the performance gap with the much larger SSPN model. The code and dataset will be released athttps://github.com/IIMARSS/FCRNafter acceptance of the paper.
