AB  - Hyperspectral imaging (HSI) has emerged as a transformative technology across diverse fields, including mineral exploration, precision agriculture, environmental surveillance, and medical diagnostics. However, the full potential of HSI classification is often constrained by three fundamental obstacles: high spectral dimensionality (the 'curse of dimensionality'), significant spectral redundancy between contiguous bands, and large interclass similarity. These challenges make it difficult to distinguish between materials with nearly identical spectral signatures, necessitating the development of robust, adversarial-resilient classification models. To overcome these concerns, this article presents a new spectral attention transformer-3D convolutional neural network (SAT-3DCNN) that combines spectral attention, multiscale three-dimensional convolution, spatial attention, and transformer-based spectral modeling into a single model. The proposed structure will jointly learn discriminative local spectral-spatial features and spectral long-range dependencies, which are typically ignored in more traditional convolution-based frameworks. Two common hyperspectral benchmark datasets, Salinas and Indian Pines, are fully evaluated to assess the performance of the proposed approach. Results of experimental studies show that SAT-3DCNN consistently outperforms state-of-the-art 2D-CNN, baseline 3D-CNN, and attention-enhanced 3D-CNN methods across all evaluation metrics. Specifically, the proposed model on the Salinas and Indian Pines datasets yields overall classification accuracies of 99.12% and 99.83%, respectively, and F1-scores over 0.99. These findings underscore the robustness and good generalization of SAT-3DCNN in handling complex spectral-spatial and class imbalances in hyperspectral imagery.
