AB  - Precision in regional short-term ionospheric modeling is essential for the reliability of satellite navigation, trans-ionospheric communication, and space weather forecasting. Due to the highly non-linear variability driven by solar activity, traditional empirical and statistical models often struggle to maintain accuracy during localized perturbations. This study develops a predictive framework using Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks to simulate short-term ionospheric dynamics across the Indian region. The model utilizes high-resolution observational data from the Meteorological and Oceanographic Satellite Data Archival Centre (MOSDAC) of the Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO). Focused on Total Electron Content (TEC) derived from ionospheric delay and frequency measurements, the LSTM architecture was designed to capture complex temporal dependencies within the diurnal cycle. Performance was rigorously evaluated against multiple baselines, including the International Reference Ionosphere (IRI-2016) and simple RNN models. The proposed LSTM model achieved a Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) of 2.10 TECU and a Mean Squared Error (MSE) of 4.41, demonstrating significantly higher accuracy and a stronger correlation (R2 = 0.95) compared to traditional empirical methods. These results establish the LSTM framework as an effective tool for short-term regional modeling and provide a scalable foundation for future data-driven research encompassing broader geomagnetic conditions and expanded spatial scales.
