N1  - Export Date: 05 October 2026
N1  - Cited By: 0
AB  - Urbanization, a major global phenomenon, has significantly transformed city structures and growth patterns, often leading to inefficient land use and dispersed growth patterns that challenge sustainable urban development. Understanding urban sprawl as a spatial pattern, not just city expansion, is important for achieving SDG 11. This study analyzes the spatial growth pattern, dispersion, and land use efficiency of Tiruchirappalli city, India, over a 20-year period (2000–2020) using integrated geospatial techniques. Landsat imagery was classified using four supervised machine learning algorithms (RF, SVM, CART and NB) to generate LULC maps for 2000, 2010, and 2020. Among these, the RF classifier achieved the highest and most consistent classification accuracy (∼95%) and was used for subsequent analysis. Urban growth patterns were examined using USM, while the degree of spatial dispersion was quantified through direction-wise and ring-wise Shannon entropy analysis. In addition, ULUE, as recommended by UN-Habitat under SDG 11.3.1, was used to assess concentration characteristics of urban growth relative to population change. The results indicate that Tiruchirappalli city experienced predominantly corridor-oriented and dispersed growth aligned with major transportation routes, reflecting a spatial structure conceptually consistent with Hoyt’s sector model. Entropy values increased over time, confirming progressive dispersion, while ULUE results showed inefficient land use during 2000–2010, followed by improved efficiency and a shift toward more compact growth during 2010–2020. By integrating spatial patterns, dispersion, and concentration metrics within a single analytical framework, this study provides a comprehensive and replicable approach for characterizing urban sprawl in medium-sized cities, offering valuable insights for sustainable urban planning.
