N1  - Export Date: 05 October 2026
N1  - Cited By: 0
AB  - Soil organic carbon (SOC) is crucial for preserving soil health, plant development, and assisting with sustainable farming methods. Developing a transferable method that can be applied across different regions for SOC estimation is vital for achieving reliable and precise measurements in various regions. There are several works on estimating SOC using multispectral or hyperspectral imagery employing various statistical or machine learning algorithms. The major limitation is the lack of studies on the modelling from a transferability perspective to apply on a realistic operational basis. A transferable model for SOC enables rapid and field-based strategies to manage soil heterogeneity. The objective of this research is to develop a site-independent, machine-learning transferable method for estimating SOC from hyperspectral data. As part of a larger perspective on soil spectroscopy, in situ, airborne, and satellite hyperspectral data were acquired at two sites in India and Germany. In situ spectral measurements and soil samples taken over the German site were used to build a reference SOC model. The hyperspectral datasets processed for retrieving spectral reflectance were used for the estimation of SOC using four machine-learning algorithms: Partial Least Square Regression (PLSR), Random Forest Regression (RFR), Elastic Net Regression (ENR), and Support Vector Regression (SVR). The quality of results was assessed using different validation metrics with reference ground truth SOC. The better-performing PLSR-based model has exhibited good retrieval with a coefficient of determination ((Formula presented) ) of 74% and a ratio of performance to deviation (RPD) of 1.66 across platforms and sites. The results suggest broad prospects of transferable ML-based models for field-level SOC mapping using hyperspectral data across regions and data acquisition platforms or sensors.
