N1  - Export Date: 05 October 2026
N1  - Cited By: 0
AB  - Semantic segmentation of high-resolution remote sensing (HRRS) images has become a hot topic but with large difficulties owing to variable target scales, similar object interference, and partial occlusion. The remarkable successes of hybrid models of convolutional neural network and transformer indicate the necessity of integrating local contexts and global dependencies. However, insufficient mining and fusion of local and global features remain the main defects for current models. To address these issues, this study proposes a novel halo transformer embedding U-Net semantic segmentation model (HaloSeg). It constitutes a parallel dual-encoder architecture by coupling halo transform with residual network for global and local expression learning separately to avoid mutual interference in hierarchical feature extraction, with a shared decoder constructed for semantic prediction based on the fused features. First, an innovative halo transformer is constructed based on an improved multiscale halo self-attention to fully exploit distant dependencies within different enlarged receptive fields and adaptively fuse them to produce expressive global features. Second, a dual attention fusion (DAF) module is developed to further enhance global and local expressions and precisely fuse their complementarity to generate informative features for each layer, thus improving model discriminability. Third, an adaptive spatial structure mining (SSM) module is designed to learn multiscale and deformable expressions from primary convolutional feature to mitigate detail loss of high-level semantic features to promote more accurate semantic prediction. The experimental results on three public HRRS datasets demonstrate that HaloSeg significantly outperforms the other state-of-the-art models and is a promising tool for semantic segmentation.
ER  -

