AB  - Semantic segmentation of remote sensing images is a core task in earth observation, yet existing convolutional neural networks based methods suffer from inefficient feature extraction via skip connections and the loss of small-target features during downsampling. This paper proposes ASG-TFANet, a hybrid network integrated with the IFDTM and HCASG modules. The IFDTM fuses Fourier and wavelet transforms to realize cross-domain interaction between global frequency-domain and local spatial-domain features, suppressing the loss of small-target features and enhancing high-frequency details. The HCASG replaces traditional skip connections and fuses multiple types of convolutions to mine valid multi-scale features and eliminate redundant information. A combined loss function integrating Dice loss, cross-entropy loss, and auxiliary cross-entropy loss is also designed to optimize inter-class discrimination. Experiments on the LoveDA and Vaihingen datasets show that the method achieves mIoU/OA of 80.20%/86.30% and 85.94%/93.95%. Visualization and ablation experiments verify that ASG-TFANet performs excellently in boundary localization, small-target segmentation, and inter-class discrimination in complex remote sensing scenes, and its modules effectively strengthen the capabilities of global-local feature interaction and multi-scale feature mining. It provides an effective solution for fine-grained remote sensing image segmentation and has broad application prospects in fields such as land use monitoring.
