AB  - The swift urbanization and administrative amalgamation among regions in Vietnam have intensified the demands on the construction area, requiring advanced vulnerability assessment tools that can capture inter-regional interactions and ecological integration. This research employed 10 m Sentinel-2 multispectral imagery alongside 317 field survey points to effectively classify land use land cover (LULC) through the application of Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN). The resultant LULC data was subsequently integrated with 16 terrain, natural, and anthropogenic variables within an Adaptive Neuro Fuzzy Inference System enhanced by an Artificial Bee Colony (ABC-ANFIS) framework, where the ABC algorithm optimized fuzzy membership functions and rule parameters to improve nonlinear predictions of vulnerability in urban development zones. The CNN model achieved remarkable classification performance, with Overall Accuracy (OA) exceeding 86% and a Kappa coefficient greater than 0.83, demonstrating its proficiency in distinguishing various cover types, especially constructed land, water features, and green areas. This investigation integrated both natural and anthropogenic terrain variables to create a vulnerability map of the construction area, attaining commendable results (R2adj = 0.83; RMSE = 5.8; and MAE = 3.72). The findings indicated that Ho Chi Minh City, the Thu Duc – Di An – Thuan An urban corridor, and Ba Ria city emerged as resilience hotspots, with values surpassing 0.8, indicative of a high concentration of infrastructure coupled with a limited capacity for ecological buffering. Conversely, peri-urban ecological areas such as Can Gio, Phu Giao, and Xuyen Moc exhibited low vulnerability levels due to their extensive forest cover, water bodies, and agricultural landscapes which collectively contribute to environmental regulation. While the 10 m spatial resolution of Sentinel-2 satellite imagery is adequate for regional-scale analyses, it may constrain the detection of micro-scale infrastructure vulnerabilities in densely constructed urban environments. Overall, this research proposed a cohesive framework that integrates remote sensing and machine learning methodologies, aimed at enhancing resilience assessments in regions that have undergone administrative consolidation, thereby providing scientific guidance for spatial planning focused on sustainability and urban resilience.
